#{[17592186147792 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$8x^{2}y^{″} + 2x(4x + 1)y^{′} + y = 0$\nWhich of the following is a Frobenius series solution to the differential equation given above, where $a_{n}$ for $n = 0, 1, 2, …$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: Substituting a trial Frobenius series solution into each term of the ODE, we obtain*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}{y}^{''} & =8({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x(4x+1){y}^{'} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}[8(\\lambda +m-1){a}_{n-1}+2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}]{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ y & ={a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151884 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the general terminology for sinusoidal curves:\n\n![](images/f6/f693f83ce4198b52d8b6dbca791cdf2855c7879dcf230193e824c23c10ede995.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\nF(t) = A \\cos\\left(B t \\right) + C\n$$\n\nthen\n\n- $\\mid A \\mid$ is the *amplitude*,\n- $\\dfrac{\\mid B \\mid}{2\\pi}$ is the *frequency,* $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\mid B \\mid}$ is the *period*, and\n- $C$ is the *midline* (vertical shift).\n\nWe'll answer each part of the question in turn.\n\n- For the first part, we are interested in the time difference between the highest and lowest tides (i.e., *half* the period of the function).Now, we have\n$B = \\frac{π}{6}$.\nSo, the period is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\pi }{B} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =12\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe time between the lowest and highest tides is *half* the period. Therefore, it takes $6$ hours for the tide to transition from its lowest to highest level.\n- For the second part, we're interested in the maximum value of the function. This is given by the amplitude plus the midline. Now, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mid A \\mid = 6\\,\\textrm{ft}, \\qquad C = 8\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle H_{\\textrm{max}} = 6\\,\\textrm{ft} + 8\\,\\textrm{ft} = 14\\,\\textrm{ft}.\n$$\nTherefore, the maximum height of the tide is $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$."] [17592186151988 :question/worked-solution "To decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=15$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse to $a=15$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 15\\cdot 1 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =26-15 \\\\ 4 & =15-11 \\\\ 3 & =11-4\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =4-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4-3 \\\\  & =4-(11-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =(15-11)\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-11\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-(26-15)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7-26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7+26\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 7 + 26 \\cdot (-4) & ≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 7 & ≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $15 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=15$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=7$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 25≡175≡19 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0≡0 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 9≡63≡11 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 20≡140≡10 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{TALK}$."] [17592186152017 :question/worked-solution "Euler's theorem states that if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\na^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{gcd}(5,39)=1$.\n- Statement II is true. First, notice that\n$39 = 3 \\cdot 13$,\nwhere $3$ and $13$ are distinct prime numbers. As a result,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ(39) & =ϕ(3)\\cdot ϕ(13) \\\\  & =(3-1)\\cdot (13-1) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Statement III is true. By Euler's theorem, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a^{ϕ(n)} & ≡1 & (modn) \\\\ 5^{ϕ(39)} & ≡1 & (mod39) \\\\ 5^{24} & ≡1 & (mod39)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186152022 :question/worked-solution "Given a natural number $n$, Euler's totient function $\\phi(n)$ returns the total number of positive integers that are less than $n$ and are coprime to $n$.\n\nRecall that Euler's totient function is a multiplicative function, meaning that if two positive integers $m$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\n\\phi(mn) = \\phi(m)\\phi(n)\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n210 = 5\\cdot 6 \\cdot 7\n$$\n\n- Since $5$ and $7$ are distinct prime numbers, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ(5) & =5-1=4, \\\\ ϕ(7) & =7-1=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- To compute $\\phi(6)$, let's write down all positive integers that are less than $6$ and cancel out the numbers that are not coprime to $6$:\n$1, \\: \\bcancel{2}, \\: \\bcancel{3}, \\bcancel{4},\\: 5$\nSo, $\\phi(6) = 2$.\n\nFinally, since $\\phi$ is multiplicative and\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(5,6,7)=1\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(210) &= ϕ(5) \\cdot ϕ(6) \\cdot ϕ(7) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186153388 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we encrypted a message using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. The resulting encrypted message was $\\textrm{VMU}$.\n\nTo decrypt this message, all we have to do is multiply by the inverse of the key:\n\n$$\na^{-1} \\cdot i_\\textrm{cipher} \\equiv i_\\textrm{plain} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nLet's recall the indices of the English alphabet and then decipher the message.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 3\\cdot 8 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =26-3\\cdot 8 \\\\ 1 & =3-2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2 \\\\  & =3-(26-3\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9-26 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9+26\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 9 + 26 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 9 & ≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $3 \\cdot 9 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=9$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=9$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\cdot 21≡189≡7 & (mod26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 12≡108≡4 & (mod26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 20≡180≡24 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nWe successfully recovered the original message, which was $\\text{HEY}$."] [17592186153475 :tutorial/content "Any decimal containing *repeating* digits is rational. This means that every decimal containing repeating digits can be written as the ratio of two integers (i.e., as a fraction).\n\nLet's see how to convert the decimal $0.3333\\ldots$ to a fraction. We proceed in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** We define our number as $x$:\n$x = 0.3333 \\ldots$\nNotice that the *repeating digit* is $3$.\n- **Step 2:** We multiply our number by $10$ to create a number with the same digits after the decimal point as $x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\times x & =10\\times 0.3333… \\\\ 10x & =3.3333…\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, we now have the following two numbers:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =3.{\\color{blue}{3333}}… \\\\ x & =0.{\\color{blue}{3333}}…\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThese two numbers have the same repeating digit after the decimal point. In other words, they have the same \"tail.\" This is crucial for the next step.\n\n- **Step 3**: We subtract $x$ from $10x$:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}10x & =3.3333… \\\\ -\\hspace{2em}x & =0.3333… \\\\ \\hline 9x & =3\\end{array}\n$$\nNotice that the tails canceled out, leaving us with the equation $9x=3$ that contains only integers.\n\n- **Step 4**: Finally, we solve the equation for $x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x & =3 \\\\ \\frac{9x}{9} & =\\frac{3}{9} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.3333 \\ldots = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$"] [17592186153476 :tutorial/content "Consider the following decimal:\n\n$$\n0.67777\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis decimal contains a *non-repeating* digit $(6)$ followed by a *repeating digit* $(7)$.\n\nTo express this decimal as a fraction, we need to follow five steps. The extra step deals with the non-repeating digit.\n\n- **Step 1:** We define our number as $x$:\n$x = 0.67777 \\ldots$\n\n- **Step 2:** Since we have one *non-repeating* digit, we multiply $x$ by $10^1 = 10$ to create a number containing only repeating digits after the decimal point. This gives\n$\\begin{aligned}10x & =6.777…\\hspace{0.222em}.\\end{aligned}$.\n\n- **Step 3**. Since we have one *repeating* digit, we multiply $10x$ by $10^{1}=10$ to create a number with the same digits after the decimal point as $10x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\times 10x & =10\\times 6.77777… \\\\ 100x & =67.777…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 4:** We subtract $10x$ from $100x$:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}100x & =67.{\\color{blue}{7777}}… \\\\ -\\hspace{2em}10x & =6.{\\color{blue}{7777}}… \\\\ \\hline 90x & =61\\end{array}\n$$\n- **Step 5:** Finally, we solve the equation for $x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90x & =61 \\\\ \\frac{90x}{90} & =\\frac{61}{90} \\\\ x & =\\frac{61}{90}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.67777 \\ldots = \\dfrac{61}{90}\n$$\n\nLet's look at an example of a decimal containing two non-repeating digits."] [17592186157017 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to show the following:\n$(20 \\mid a) \\: \\land\\: (21 \\mid a) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 35 \\mid a$\n\nFirst, we recall the following fact:\n$(d \\mid a) \\:\\land\\: (b \\mid d) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad b \\mid a$\nTherefore, we start our proof as follows:\n\n> Because $5 \\mid 20$ and $7 \\mid 21$, we have\n> $\\begin{bmatrix}20∣a & ⇒5∣a, \\\\ 21∣a & ⇒7∣a\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nNotice that $5$ and $7$ are coprime, which means their greatest common divisor is $1{:}$\n\n> Since $5$ and $7$ are coprime, we have $\\gcd(5, 7) = 1$.\n\nWe now use Bézout’s identity:\n\n> By Bézout’s identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n> $5u+7v = 1$.\n\nSince we want to relate $a$ with $5$ and $7$, we multiply both sides by $a{:}$\n\n> Multiplying both sides by $a$, we get\n> $5ua+7va = a$.\n\nWe now use the fact that $5\\mid a$ and $7 \\mid a$, along with the definition of divisibility.\n\n> Since $5 \\mid a$ and $7 \\mid a$, there exist integers $k$ and $m$ such that $a = 5k$ and $a = 7m$.\n\nWe use these expressions for $a$ to rewrite the left-hand side of the equality above, obtaining an expression relating $35$ and $a{:}$\n\n> Substituting these for $a$ into the left-hand side of the expression above, we obtain\n> $\\begin{aligned}a & =5ua+7va \\\\  & =5u(7m)+7v(5k) \\\\  & =35um+35vk \\\\  & =35(um+vk).\\end{aligned}$.\n\nBy the definition of divisibility once more, we have that $35$ divides $a{:}$\n\n> Since $um+vk$ is an integer, we conclude that $35 \\mid a$."] [17592186157056 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{a is divisible by 10} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm{a is divisible by 2}\n$$\n\nRecall that if an integer $a$ is divisible by $d$, then there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\na = d \\cdot k\n$$\n\nUsing this definition, we begin our proof as follows:\n\n> By the definition of integer divisibility, if $a$ is divisible by $10$, then\n> $a = 10k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nNow, we'll rewrite our expression to explicitly show that $2$ is a factor of $a{:}$\n\n> Now, note that\n> $\\begin{aligned}a & =10k \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5\\cdot k \\\\  & =2(5k) \\\\  & =2m\\end{aligned}$\n> where $m=5k$ is an integer.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of integer divisibility once more:\n\n> Therefore, by the definition of integer divisibility, since $a=2m$, we conclude that $a$ is divisible by $2$."] [17592186157057 :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to show the following:\n$(4 \\mid a) \\: \\land\\: (15 \\mid a) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 60 \\mid a$\n\nNotice that $4$ and $15$ are coprime, which means their greatest common divisor is $1{:}$\n\n> Since $4$ and $15$ are coprime, we have $\\gcd(4, 15) = 1$.\n\nWe now use Bézout’s identity:\n\n> By Bézout’s identity, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n> $4u+15v = 1$.\n\nSince we want to relate $a$ with $4$ and $15$, we multiply both sides by $a{:}$\n\n> Multiplying both sides by $a$, we get\n> $4ua+15va = a$.\n\nWe use now the fact that $4\\mid a$ and $15 \\mid a$, along with the definition of divisibility.\n\n> Since $4 \\mid a$ and $15 \\mid a$, there exists integers $k$ and $m$ such that $a = 4k$ and $a = 15m$.\n\nWe use these expressions for $a$ to rewrite the left-hand side of the equality above, obtaining an expression relating $60$ and $a{:}$\n\n> Substituting these expressions for $a$ into the left-hand side of the expression above, we obtain\n> $\\begin{aligned}a & =4ua+15va \\\\  & =4u(15m)+15v(4k) \\\\  & =60um+60vk \\\\  & =60(um+vk).\\end{aligned}$.\n\nBy the definition of divisibility once more, we have that $60$ divides $a{:}$\n\n> Since $um+vk$ is an integer, we conclude that $60 \\mid a$."] [17592186157058 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n3n-1 \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 3n-1 \\text{is odd}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\n> If $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $3n-1$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}3n-1 & =3(2a)-1 \\\\  & =6a-1 \\\\  & =(6a-2)+1 \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(3a-1)}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=3a-1$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n3n-1 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $3n-1$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b=3a-1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\begin{aligned}3n-1 & =2\\underbrace{(3a-1)}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1,\\end{aligned}$,\n> which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $3n-1$ is odd."] [17592186157059 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $a$ such that $n = 2a$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $a$ such that $n = 2a+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n$n^3 + 1 \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n \\text{is odd}$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad n^3 + 1 \\text{is odd}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\n> If $n$ is even, then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n = 2a$ into the expression $n^3 + 1$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+1 & =(2a)^{3}+1 \\\\  & =8{a}^{3}+1 \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(4{a}^{3})}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b = 4a^3$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nn^3+1 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $n^3+1$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $b = 4a^3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+1 & =2\\underbrace{(4{a}^{3})}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1,\\end{aligned}$,\n> which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $n^3+1$ is odd."] [17592186157061 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional statement $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and its contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nare logically equivalent. Moreover, it's sometimes easier to prove the contrapositive.\n\nIn this case, we need to show that\n$\\underbrace{\\textrm{a is not divisible by 6}}_{P} \n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \n\\underbrace{\\textrm{a is not divisible by 42}}_{Q}.$\nThe contrapositive form of this statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big( \\textrm{a is not divisible by 42} \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \n\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big( \\textrm{a is not divisible by 6} \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P},$\nwhich is equivalent to\n$\\textrm{a is divisible by 42} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm{a is divisible by 6}.$\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> The contrapositive of the statement is\n> $\\textrm{a is divisible by 42} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm{a is divisible by 6}.$\n> We will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nRecall that if an integer $a$ is divisible by $d$, then there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\na = d \\cdot k\n$$\n\nUsing this definition, we continue the proof as follows:\n\n> By the definition of integer divisibility, if $a$ is divisible by $42$, then\n> $a = 42k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nNow, we'll rewrite our expression to explicitly show that $6$ is a factor of $a{:}$\n\n> Now, note that\n> $\\begin{aligned}a & =42k \\\\  & =6\\cdot 7\\cdot k \\\\  & =6(7k) \\\\  & =6m\\end{aligned}$\n> where $m = 7k$ is an integer.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of integer divisibility once more:\n\n> Therefore, by the definition of integer divisibility, since $a = 6m$, we conclude that $a$ is divisible by $6$."] [17592186157064 :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, to prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it is easier to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nthat is equivalent to the initial implication.\n\nIn this case, we need to show that\n$\\underbrace{a+7 \\not\\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)}_{Q}$.\nThe contrapositive of the statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(a+7 \\not\\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}$,\nwhich is equivalent to\n$a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a+7 \\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$.\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> The contrapositive of the statement is\n> $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a+7 \\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$.\n> We will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the definition of congruence modulo $n$. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$. So, we can reason as follows:\n\n> Since $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$, by the definition of modular congruence,\n> $10 \\mid (a-b) \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad a-b = 10k$\n> for some integer $k$.\n\nNow, we express the difference between $a+7$ and $b+7$ through the difference between $a$ and $b{:}$\n\n> Now, notice that\n> $\\begin{aligned}(a+7)-(b+7) & =a+7-b-7 \\\\  & =a-b \\\\  & =10k.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence in reverse:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have\n> $a+7 \\equiv b+7 \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 10)$.\n> By proving the contrapositive, we proved the initial statement, as required."] [17592186157125 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the set of solutions in the non-negative integers with $x > 9$ and let $B$ be the set of solutions in the non-negative integers with\n\n$$\ny \\geq 7\n$$\n\nThen the number of solutions where $x > 9$ or\n\n$$\ny \\geq 7\n$$\n\ncorresponds to $\\mid A \\cup B \\mid$, and we can compute this using the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\n$$\n|A\\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A\\cap B|\n$$\n\n- To compute $\\mid A \\mid$, we need to count the number of solutions in the non-negative integers such that $x > 9$, or equivalently, $x \\geq 10$. To do this, we can replace $x=10+x'$, where $x'$ is a non-negative integer, which gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =16 \\\\ (10+{x}^{'})+y+z & =16 \\\\ {x}^{'}+y+z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the number of solutions in $A$ is equal to the number of non-negative integer solutions of the equation above, which is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6+3-1}{6})=\\frac{8!}{6!2!}=28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- To compute $\\mid B \\mid$, we apply the same procedure. We replace $y=7+y'$ to get the equation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =16 \\\\ x+(7+{y}^{'})+z & =16 \\\\ x+{y}^{'}+z & =9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand the number of solutions of this equation is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B| & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9+3-1}{9})=\\frac{11!}{9!2!}=55.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- To compute $\\mid A\\cap B \\mid$, we need to find the number of solutions in the non-negative integers such that both $x > 9$ and $y \\geq 7$. So, we replace both $x=10+x'$ and $y=7+y'$ and get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =16 \\\\ (10+{x}^{'})+(7+{y}^{'})+z & =16 \\\\ {x}^{'}+{y}^{'}+z & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich permits no non-negative integer solutions. This tells us that $\\mid A \\cap B \\mid = 0$.\n\nFinally, according to the inclusion-exclusion principle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A\\cup B| & =|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B| \\\\  & =28+55-0 \\\\  & =83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $83$ solutions in the non-negative integers with $x > 9$ or\n\n$$\ny \\geq 7\n$$"] [17592186157126 :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ be the set of solutions of\n\n$$\nx + y + z = 17\n$$\n\nin the non-negative integers (without restriction). The number of solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle | X | & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{17 + 3 - 1}{17} \\right) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{19 !}{17 ! 2 !}} = 171.\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHowever, we only want to count the solutions in the non-negative integers with\n\n$$\nx \\leq 10\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\leq 12\n$$\n\nSo, we need to subtract the number of solutions where $x > 10$ or $y > 12$.\n\nLet $A$ be the set of solutions in the non-negative integers with $x > 10$ and let $B$ be the set of solutions in the non-negative integers with $y > 12$. Then the number of solutions where $x > 10$ or $y > 12$ corresponds to $\\mid A \\cup B \\mid$, and we can compute this using the inclusion-exclusion principle:\n\n$$\n|A\\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A\\cap B|\n$$\n\n- To compute $\\mid A \\mid$, we need to count the number of solutions in the non-negative integers such that $x > 10$, or equivalently, $x \\geq 11$. To do this, we can replace $x=11+x'$, where $x'$ is a non-negative integer, which gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =17 \\\\ (11+{x}^{'})+y+z & =17 \\\\ {x}^{'}+y+z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the number of solutions in $A$ is equal to the number of non-negative integer solutions of the equation above, which is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6+3-1}{6})=\\frac{8!}{6!2!}=28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- To compute $\\mid B \\mid$, we apply the same procedure. We replace $y=13+y'$ to get the equation\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =17 \\\\ x+(13+{y}^{'})+z & =17 \\\\ x+{y}^{'}+z & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nand the number of solutions of this equation is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B| & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4+3-1}{4})=\\frac{6!}{4!2!}=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- To compute $\\mid A\\cap B \\mid$, we need to find the number of solutions in the non-negative integers such that both $x > 10$ and $y > 12$. So, we replace both $x=11+x'$ and $y=13+y'$ and get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =17 \\\\ (11+{x}^{'})+(13+{y}^{'})+z & =17 \\\\ {x}^{'}+{y}^{'}+z & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich permits no non-negative integer solutions. This tells us that $\\mid A \\cap B \\mid = 0$.\n\nFinally, according to the inclusion-exclusion principle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A\\cup B| & =|A|+|B|-|A\\cap B| \\\\  & =28+15-0 \\\\  & =43,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the number of solutions in the non-negative integers with\n\n$$\nx \\leq 10\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\leq 12\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n|X|-|A\\cup B|=171 - 43 =128.\n$$"] [17592186157221 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hexadecimal integers $\\textrm{A}-\\textrm{F}$ correspond to decimal integers $10-15{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\textrm{A}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\textrm{B}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\textrm{C}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\textrm{D}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\textrm{E}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\textrm{F}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo write a hexadecimal integer as the corresponding binary number, we convert each digit of the hexadecimal number into a $4$-digit binary (with leading zeros if necessary) and concatenate the results.\n\n- Converting each digit of $(\\textrm{FE}9)_{16}$ into binary, we obtain:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{F} & =15={2}^{3}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{1}+{2}^{0}=(1111)_{2} \\\\ \\text{E} & =14={2}^{3}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{1}=(1110)_{2} \\\\ 9 & ={2}^{3}+{2}^{0}=(1001)_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Concatenating the results, we get\n$(\\textrm{FE}9)_{16} = (1111 \\: 1110 \\: 1001)_2$."] [17592186158569 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following statement:\n\n> *If $d \\mid 15$, then $d \\mid 60$*\n\nWe begin by writing this statement as an implication:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{d \\mid 15}_{P(d)} \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad \\underbrace{d \\mid 60}_{Q(d)}\n$$\n\nIt's clear from the context that the universal set of $P$ and $Q$ is given by $d\\in \\mathbb Z\\setminus \\{0\\}$. In other words, $d$ must be a nonzero integer.\n\nAccording to our definition of divisibility, an integer $a$ is divisible by $d$ if there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\na = d \\cdot k\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by assuming that $P(d)$ is true and applying the definition of divisibility.\n\n> *Suppose $d\\mid 15$. By the definition of integer divisibility, we have*\n> $d \\mid 15 \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad 15 = d \\cdot k$\n> *for some integer $k$.*\n\nSince we need to consider divisors of $60$ instead of $15$, let's multiply the last equation by $4{:}$\n\n> *Multiplying both sides of the equation $15 = dk$ by $4$, we obtain the following chain of implications:*\n> $15 = dk \\mid ⇒4 \\cdot 15 = 4 \\cdot dk; ⇒60 = 4dk; ⇒60 = d \\cdot 4k$\n\nNow, we need to use the definition of divisibility once more to show that $d$ divides $60$. To do this, we need to write\n\n$$\n60=d\\cdot m\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is an integer. So, we simply define a new integer $m = 4k$ and continue our proof.\n\n> $\\begin{aligned}60=d\\cdot 4k\\hspace{2em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{2em}60 & =dm\\end{aligned}$\n> *where $m=4k$ is an integer.*\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, by the definition of integer divisibility, since $60=dm$, we have that $d \\mid 60$.*\n\nLet's now state the full proof."] [17592186158571 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following proposition using the contrapositive:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3n+2 \\text{is odd}}_{P(n)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{n \\text{is odd}}_{Q(n)}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> *The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:*\n> $\\underbrace{n \\text{is even}}_{\\lnot Q(n)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{3n+2 \\text{is even}}_{\\lnot P(n)}$\n> We will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\n> *If $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.*\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $3n+2$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Therefore,*\n> $\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =3(2a)+2 \\\\  & =6a+2 \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(3a+1)}_{b}.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nb=3a+1\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n3n+2 = 2b\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $3n+2$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Let $b=3a+1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.*\n> *Hence, we have*\n> $\\begin{aligned}3n+2 & =2\\underbrace{(3a+1)}_{b} \\\\  & =2b,\\end{aligned}$,\n> *which is a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, $3n+2$ is even.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's state the full proof."] [17592186158582 :tutorial/content "Recall that the conditional statement and its contrapositive are logically equivalent.\n\n$$\nP \\Rightarrow Q \\equiv \\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nWe can use the contrapositive to prove statements about divisibility. To demonstrate, let's prove the following proposition using the contrapositive:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{d \\not\\mid 72}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{d \\not\\mid 8}_{Q}\n$$\n\nThe contrapositive form of this statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(d \\not\\mid 8 \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(d \\not\\mid 72 \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}$,\nwhich is equivalent to\n$d \\mid 8 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad d \\mid 72$.\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> *The contrapositive of the statement is*\n> $d \\mid 8 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad d \\mid 72$.\n> *We will prove this statement instead of the original one.*\n\nRecall that if an integer $a$ is divisible by $d$, then there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\na = d \\cdot k\n$$\n\nUsing this definition, we continue our proof as follows:\n\n> *By the definition of integer divisibility, we have*\n> $d \\mid 8 \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad 8 = d \\cdot k$\n> *for some integer $k$.*\n\nSince we need to consider divisors of $72$ instead of $8$, we multiply the last equation by $9{:}$\n\n> *Multiplying both sides of $8 = d k$ by $9$, we obtain*\n> $\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot 8 & =9\\cdot dk \\\\ 72 & =9dk \\\\ 72 & =d(9k) \\\\ 72 & =dm\\end{aligned}$\n> *where $m = 9k$ is an integer.*\n\nFinally, we use the definition of integer divisibility once more:\n\n> *Therefore, by the definition of integer divisibility, $72 = dm$ means that $d \\mid 72$.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and its proof."] [17592186158598 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n$\\underbrace{d \\not\\mid 72}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{d \\not\\mid 8}_{Q}$.\n\nProof:\n\n> *The contrapositive of the statement is*\n> $d \\mid 8 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad d \\mid 72$.\n> *We will prove this statement instead of the original one.*\n> *By the definition of integer divisibility, we have*\n> $d \\mid 8 \\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad 8 = d \\cdot k$\n> *for some integer $k$.*\n> *Multiplying both sides of $8 = d k$ by $9$, we obtain*\n> $\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot 8 & =9\\cdot dk \\\\ 72 & =9dk \\\\ 72 & =d(9k) \\\\ 72 & =dm\\end{aligned}$\n> *where $m = 9k$ is an integer.*\n> *Therefore, by the definition of integer divisibility, $72 = dm$ means that $d \\mid 72$.*"] [17592186163180 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves how to estimate $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ from sample data.\n\nSuppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable. A random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Observations $(y_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(O_i)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\leq y_i < 3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3\\leq y_i < 5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5\\leq y_i < 7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7\\leq y_i < 9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSuppose we wish to test the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ $Y\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ $Y\\not\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$\n\nWe're not given the mean $\\mu$ nor the variance $\\sigma^2$, so we have to estimate them from the sample data.\n\n- For the mean, we have the following estimate:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{\\mu } & =\\frac{\\text{weighted sum of the midpoints}}{\\text{total number of observations}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{N}{\\sum }_{i}{O}_{i}\\cdot {y}_{i}^{\\text{mid}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the variance, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\widehat{\\sigma}^2 = \\dfrac{1}{N-1}\\sum_i O_i \\cdot (y_i^{\\textrm{mid}} - \\widehat{\\mu})^2\n$$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\mu}$ is our estimate for the mean $\\mu$, $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2$ is our estimate for the variance $\\sigma^2$,$N$ is the total number of observations, $y_i^{\\textrm{mid}}$ is the midpoint of an observational class $(a_i, b_i)$.\n\nLet's compute estimates for the mean and variance in this case:\n\nThe total number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 8+12+11+9= 40\n$$\n\nNext, we find the midpoint of each observational class.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Observations $(y_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Frequency $(O_i)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_i^{\\textrm{mid}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\leq y_i < 3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3\\leq y_i < 5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5\\leq y_i < 7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7\\leq y_i < 9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOur estimate $\\widehat{\\mu}$ for the mean $\\mu$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\widehat{μ} &= \\frac{1}{N}∑O_{i} \\cdot y_{i}^{mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{40}(8 \\cdot 2 + 12 \\cdot 4 + 11 \\cdot 6 + 9 \\cdot 8) \\\\\n&= 5.05\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our estimate $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2$ for the variance $\\sigma^2$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{N - 1}} \\sum  {O}_{i} \\cdot  ( {y}_{i}^{\\text{mid}} - \\hat{\\mu } {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{40 - 1}} ( 8 \\cdot  ( 2 - 5.05 {)}^{2} + 12 \\cdot  ( 4 - 5.05 {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} + 11 \\cdot  ( 6 - 5.05 {)}^{2} + 9 \\cdot  ( 8 - 5.05 {)}^{2} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4.51025 \\ldots  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}4.5}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nWhen conducting a chi-square test, we lose a degree of freedom for every estimated parameter:\n\n- If *only one* parameter must be estimated, then we have two constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 2$.\n- If *both* parameters must be estimated, then we have three constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 3$.\n\nLet's see an example."] [17592186163371 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a coin that we suspect is biased *against* landing on heads (i.e., the coin is *less* likely to land on heads than tails). We wish to design a hypothesis test to determine whether or not our suspicions are correct.\n\nWe can use a so-called **left-tailed** hypothesis test for this purpose. To do that, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Determine the population parameter we're interested in. $\\qquad$ *We define $p$ as \"the probability that the coin lands on heads when tossed randomly.\"*\n- **Step 2**: Define our hypotheses:*Since we want to check whether the coin is biased **against** landing on heads, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{0}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}p & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {H}_{1}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}p & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$We use a \"less than\" symbol (the left tail of the probability distribution) for $H_1$ because if the coin is biased against heads, then the probability that the coin lands on heads must be less than one-half.\n- **Step 3**: Design a suitable experiment to test our hypotheses. $\\qquad$ *We toss our coin $10$ times and count the total number of heads.*\n- **Step 4**: Set a significance level $\\alpha$. $\\qquad$ *For our experiment, let's pick the significance level $\\alpha = 5\\%$.*\n- **Step 5**: Define and calculate a test statistic.In our case, we might define a suitable test statistic as follows:$\\qquad X=$ \"*the number of times the coin lands on heads after $10$ random tosses*.\"Notice that under the conditions specified in the null hypothesis, the random variable $X$ must follow a binomial distribution:\n$X \\sim B\\left(10,\\dfrac12\\right)$ Suppose we toss the coin $10$ times and get only ${\\color{red}{1}}$ head. If the null hypothesis is true, then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {\\color{red}{1}}) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0}){\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{0}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1}){\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{9} \\\\  & \\approx 0.0107 \\\\  & =1.07\\mathrm{\\%}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 6**: Draw a conclusion.The probability $P(X\\leq {\\color{red}{1}})$ is *smaller than* our significance level of $5\\%$. This means that our result *is* statistically significant. In other words, it is unlikely that this event could have occurred if the null hypothesis is true. Therefore, we should *reject* the null hypothesis and conclude that the coin is biased.\n\nYou might be wondering why our test statistic was\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq {\\color{red}{1}})\n$$\n\nrather than\n\n$$\nP(X={\\color{red}{1}})\n$$\n\nWhen conducting hypothesis tests, we consider not just the observed outcome, but all outcomes that are *at least as extreme*—meaning outcomes that point even more strongly toward the alternative hypothesis. Since our alternative hypothesis is\n\n$$\np < \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nlower values of $X$ provide stronger evidence for it. The cumulative probability\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq 1)\n$$\n\naccounts for $X = 1$ and the even more extreme outcome $X = 0$.\n\n**Right-tailed** hypothesis tests are similar. The difference is the alternative hypothesis involves a \"greater than\" sign. Let's see an example."] [17592186167199 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ}y & ={y}^{'}\\cdot \\mathrm{Δ}x \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\mathrm{Δ}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf the exact solution is $y_{exact}(x) = x^{2}$, find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to $x_{new} = x + Δx$."] [17592186167201 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{′} = 2y, y(0) = 1\n$$\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ}y & ={y}^{'}\\cdot \\mathrm{Δ}x \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\mathrm{Δ}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\nGiven that the exact solution is $y_{exact}(x) = e^{2x}$, compute the local truncation error $τ_{1}$ for the first step starting at $x = 0$ with step size $Δx = 0.1$. Round your answer to three decimal places.$τ_{1} \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167202 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y^{′} = x + 1, y(0) = 0\n$$\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{Δ}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{y}^{'})\\cdot \\mathrm{Δ}x \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\mathrm{Δ}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\nGiven that the exact solution is $y_{exact}(x) = \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + x$, compute the local truncation error, $τ_{1}$, for the first step starting at $x = 0$ with step size $Δx = 0.2$.$τ_{1} =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186167542 :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{T}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{t},-\\sqrt{1-t},0⟩, \\\\ \\mathbf{N}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{1-t},\\sqrt{t},0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."] [17592186171691 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\sin{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/08/08d442eff9febe44bd39e6c15c998d76d56d17bbb7a676d2d511771479bb2f29.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sin{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\sin{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n$\\sin(-x) = -\\sin{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(-x+16\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 8) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(-x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}x \\\\  & =-\\left(-\\frac{4}{7}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin(-x + 16\\pi) = \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$"] [17592186171734 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP: \\quad \\forall x, y\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 6x-9y \\neq 2\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P: \\quad \\exists x, y\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 6x-9y = 2\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that there exists $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $6x-9y = 2$.\n\nThe idea is to divide both sides by a specific number and show that the left-hand side is an integer while the right-hand side is not.\n\nSince $6$ and $9$ have a common factor of $3$, we can divide the left-hand side by this factor without introducing fractional coefficients.\n\n> Dividing both sides by $3$ gives\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-9y & =2 \\\\ \\frac{6x-9y}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{6x}{3}-\\frac{9y}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 2x-3y & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we show that we've reached a contradiction.\n\n> Since $x$ and $y$ are integers, we have that $2x - 3y$ is an integer.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\dfrac23$ is not an integer.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our assumption that $\\lnot P$ is true is incorrect. Hence, $P$ is true, and we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $6x-9y \\neq 2$ for any $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$."] [17592186171735 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{n^4-1\\textrm{is even}}_{P}\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{n\\textrm{is odd}}_{Q}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P\\Rightarrow Q$ is true, we assume $P$ and $\\lnot Q$ are both true and show that this leads to a contradiction.\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $n^4-1$ is even and $n$ is even. Then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into the expression $n^4-1$ and obtain a contradiction.\n\n> As a result,\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{4}-1 & =(2a)^{4}-1 \\\\  & =16{a}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =(16{a}^{4}-2)+1 \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(8{a}^{4}-1)}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we define another integer $b=8a^4-1$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nn^4-1 = 2b+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $n^4-1$ is one more than a multiple of $2$. But since $n^4-1$ is even, we've reached a contradiction.\n\n> Let $b=8a^4-1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> So, we have\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{4}-1 & =2\\underbrace{(8{a}^{4}-1)}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1,\\end{aligned}$,\n> which is one larger than a multiple of $2$. But this is a contradiction since $n^4-1$ is even.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our assumption that $\\lnot Q$ is true is incorrect. Hence, $Q$ is true, and we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $n$ is odd."] [17592186171745 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the definition of an injection:\n\n> A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is an injection if every element $x\\in X$ is mapped to a distinct element in $Y{:}$\n> $x_1 \\neq x_2 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad f(x_1) \\neq f(x_2)$\n\nIn this case, it would be easier to prove the contrapositive statement.\n\n> This implication is equivalent to its contrapositive:\n> $f(x_1)=f(x_2) \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad x_1=x_2$\n\nNow, we simply need to derive $z_1=z_2$ by assuming that\n\n$$\nf(z_1)=f(z_2){:}\n$$\n\n> So, let $z_1,z_2 \\in \\mathbb{C}$, and suppose that $f(z_1)=f(z_2)$. Then, we have\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}f({z}_{1}) & =f({z}_{2}) \\\\ (\\frac{1}{2}({z}_{1}+\\overset{―}{{z}_{1}}),\\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{2}(\\overset{―}{{z}_{1}}-{z}_{1})) & =(\\frac{1}{2}({z}_{2}+\\overset{―}{{z}_{2}}),\\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{2}(\\overset{―}{{z}_{2}}-{z}_{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> Since two pairs are equal if and only if their respective components are equal, we obtain:\n> $$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} ( {z}_{1} + \\overline{ {z}_{1}} ) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} ( {z}_{2} + \\overline{ {z}_{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{2}} ( \\overline{ {z}_{1}} - {z}_{1} ) = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\mathrm{i}}{2}} ( \\overline{ {z}_{2}} - {z}_{2} )\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle  \\Rightarrow  \\hspace{2em} \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle {z}_{1} + \\overline{ {z}_{1}} = {z}_{2} + \\overline{ {z}_{2}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\overline{ {z}_{1}} - {z}_{1} = \\overline{ {z}_{2}} - {z}_{2}\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}}\n$$\n> To solve this system, recall the following properties of the complex conjugate:\n> $z_1+\\overline{z_1} = 2\\cdot \\textrm{Re}(z_1), \\qquad z_1-\\overline{z_1} = 2\\textrm{i}\\cdot \\textrm{Im}(z_1)$\n> Therefore, our system reduces to\n> $$\n{\\begin{array}{rlr}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 \\cdot  \\text{Re} ( {z}_{1} ) = 2 \\cdot  \\text{Re} ( {z}_{2} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle - 2 \\text{i} \\cdot  \\text{Im} ( {z}_{1} ) = - 2 \\text{i} \\cdot  \\text{Im} ( {z}_{2} )\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle  \\Rightarrow  \\hspace{1em} \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle \\text{Re} ( {z}_{1} ) = \\text{Re} ( {z}_{2} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( {z}_{1} ) = \\text{Im} ( {z}_{2} )\\end{array} \\right.  \\hspace{1em} & \\displaystyle  \\Rightarrow  \\hspace{1em} {z}_{1} = {z}_{2}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state the conclusion:\n\n> Hence, $z_1=z_2$, as was to be shown. Therefore, $f$ is an injection."] [17592186171749 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the definition of a surjection:\n\n> A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is a surjection if, for every element $y$ in $Y$, there exists an element in $X$ that maps to $y{:}$\n> $\\forall \\, y \\in Y, \\, \\exists \\, x \\in X, \\: f(x)=y$\n\nThe idea is to take an arbitrary $y \\in Y$ and show that we can always find at least one value of $x$ that is mapped to $y$ under $f{:}$\n\n> So, let $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then, we have the following:\n> $\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =y \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =y\\end{aligned}$\n> For example, if we take $x_1=\\dfrac{y}{3}$ and $x_2=0$, then $f$ maps $(x_1,x_2)$ to $y$.\n\nFinally, we state the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $f$ is a surjection."] [17592186171750 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the definition of a surjection:\n\n> A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is a surjection if, for every element $y$ in $Y$, there exists an element in $X$ that maps to $y{:}$\n> $\\forall \\, y \\in Y, \\, \\exists \\, x \\in X, \\: f(x)=y$\n\nThe negation of this statement is\n\n$$\n\\exists \\, y \\in Y, \\, \\forall \\, x \\in X, \\: f(x) \\neq y\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following:\n\n> To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample.\n> In other words, we need to show that there exists $y$ such that\n> $f(x) \\neq y$\n> for any possible $x$.\n\nWe simply need to find a value $y$ that does not have a preimage.\n\n> As a counterexample, let's take $y=(1,0)$. Notice that\n> $\\begin{aligned}f(m) & =y \\\\ (m,3m-1) & =(1,0)\\end{aligned}$\n> Since two pairs are equal if and only if their respective components are equal, we obtain the system\n> ${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}m = 1 \\\\ 3 m - 1 = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$\n> which has no solution.\n\nFinally, we state the conclusion:\n\n> Hence, the pair $(1,0)$ has no preimage in $\\mathbb{Z}$. Therefore, $f$ isn't a surjection."] [17592186171752 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP: \\quad \\forall x, y\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 45x+27y \\neq 1\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P: \\quad \\exists x, y\\in\\mathbb Z,\\,45x+27y = 1\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that there exists $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $45x+27y = 1$.\n\nThe idea is to factor the expression on the left-hand side and consider the product as factors of the integer on the right-hand side.\n\n> Factoring the expression on the left-hand side gives\n> $\\begin{aligned}45x+27y & =1 \\\\ 9\\underbrace{(5x+3y)}_{a} & =1.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\na=5x+3y\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have $9a=1$, which, by the definition of divisibility, means $9 \\:\\mid\\: 1$.\n\n> Let $a =5x+3y$. Since $x$ and $y$ are integers, we have that $a$ is an integer.\n> Hence, we have\n> $\\begin{aligned}9\\underbrace{(5x+3y)}_{a} & =1 \\\\ 9a & =1,\\end{aligned}$,\n> which means $9 \\:\\mid\\: 1$. But this is a contradiction since $9 \\:\\nmid\\: 1$.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our assumption that $\\lnot P$ is true is incorrect. Hence, $P$ is true, and we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $45x+27y \\neq 1$ for any $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$."] [17592186171791 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a contingency table with $n$ rows and $m$ columns. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\sum_{j} \\frac{(O_{ij} - E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_{ij}$ is the observed frequency for row $i$ and column $j$, and\n- $E_{ij}$ is the expected frequency for row $i$ and column $j$, as predicted under the null hypothesis that the row and column factors are independent.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_{ij}$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_{ij} \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each cell), and the observations are independent.\n\nNotice that all of the expected frequencies are greater than or equal to $5$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(O_{ij} - E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\n$$\n\nfor each combination of values of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((O_{11} - E_{11})^{2})/(E_{11}) &= ((37 - 33.2)^{2})/(33.2) \\approx 0.4349 \\\\\n((O_{12} - E_{12})^{2})/(E_{12}) &= ((3 - 6.8)^{2})/(6.8) \\approx 2.1235 \\\\\n((O_{21} - E_{21})^{2})/(E_{21}) &= ((46 - 49.8)^{2})/(49.8) \\approx 0.2900 \\\\\n((O_{22} - E_{22})^{2})/(E_{22}) &= ((14 - 10.2)^{2})/(10.2) \\approx 1.4157\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we rounded to four decimal places.\n\nFirst, we compute our test statistic by summing the above results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= ∑_(i)∑_(j)((O_{ij} - E_{ij})^{2})/(E_{ij}) \\\\\n&= 0.4349 + 2.1235 + 0.2900 + 1.4157 \\\\\n&\\approx 4.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4.3}$.\n\nWe now compute the number of degrees of freedom:\n\n- Given a contingency table with $n$ rows and $m$ columns: the number of constraints is $1 + (m-1) + (n-1)$ the total number of observations must equal $N$, and the estimates $\\hat p (A_1), \\hat p(A_2),\\ldots \\hat p(A_{n-1})$ and $\\hat p(B_1), \\hat p(B_2), \\ldots \\hat p(B_{m-1})$ set a constraint each. Notice that the $\\hat p(A_n)$ and $\\hat p(B_m)$ can be deduced by the others, so they don't create a constraint. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ν & =\\text{number of cells}-\\text{number of constraints} \\\\  & =nm-1-(n-1)-(m-1) \\\\  & =nm-n-m+1 \\\\  & =(n-1)(m-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, in our case there are $\\nu = (2-1)(2-1)=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ degree of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=1$ at a $5\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=3.841\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 3.841\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f17c7174d7df5c99801af4db09cdb840b0471f6254e757575f4d59110369926d.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(4.3)$ lies inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis."] [17592186171802 :question/worked-solution "Let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. In general, if $Y = X_1 + X_2$, where $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables with MGF's $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$, respectively, then the MGF of $Y$ is\n$M_Y(t) = M_{X_1}(t) M_{X_2}(t)$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $Y = X_1 + X_2$, we know that $Y$ and $X_1+X_2$ have the same probability distribution. We represent this as $Y \\stackrel{d}{=} X_1 + X_2$.\n- Statement III is true. A normally distributed random variable $X \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ has the following MGF:\n$M(t) = e^{\\mu t + \\sigma^2 t^2/2}, \\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R$\nSo the MGF's for $X_1 \\sim N(5, 4)$ and $X_2 \\sim N(3, 2)$ are\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = e^{5t + 2t^2}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = e^{3t + t^2},\n$$\nand we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t){M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{5t+2{t}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{3t+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{8t+3{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $M_Y(t)$ is the MGF for the distribution $N(8, 6)$, we conclude that $Y \\sim N(8, 6)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186173132 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the example of the distribution of the daily incomes of three shops.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group 2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Group 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10.8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We will denote the number of groups as $K$. In our case, $K=3$.\n- Each group will be identified with a number $k$ between $1$ and $K$.\n- For each group, we will denote the number of data points in the group as $n_k$.\nIn our case, we reported the incomes of $4$ days for each shop. So, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle n_1 = 4, \\quad n_2 = 4, \\quad n_3 = 4.\n$$\n- We denote $x_{jk}$ the $j$th data point from the $k$th group. So, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11} & =15,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{21}=17,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{31}=16,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{41}=14, \\\\ {x}_{12} & =12.5,\\hspace{2em}{x}_{22}=11.5,\\hspace{2em}{x}_{32}=13,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{42}=11, \\\\ {x}_{13} & =9,\\phantom{5.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{23}=11,\\phantom{.5}\\hspace{2em}{x}_{33}=10.8,\\hspace{2em}{x}_{43}=9.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- We denote $\\overline{x}_{k}$ the sample mean of the $k$th group:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\overline{x}_{1} = 15.5, \\qquad \\overline{x}_{2} = 12, \\qquad \\overline{x}_{3} = 10\n$$"] [17592186173134 :tutorial/content "Let's return to the following contingency table, which includes the observed and expected frequencies for a sample of $N=60$ students who sat an exam from two schools.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Pass</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Fail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">School 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$O_{11}=18$<br>$E_{11} =16.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$O_{12}=6$<br>$E_{12} =7.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">School 2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$O_{18}=24$<br>$E_{21} =25.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$O_{12}=12$<br>$E_{22} =10.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nRecall that for a chi-square goodness-of-fit test, the number of degrees of freedom is the number of independent sample categories (cells). The case for independence tests is similar:\n\n- In this case, we have a contingency table with $2\\cdot 2 = 4$ cells.\n- In total, we have $3$ constraints:The sum of the observations must equal $N$.We also formed two estimates, which both set an additional constraint:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\hat p (\\textrm{Students is from School 1}) = 0.4, \\qquad \\hat p (\\textrm{Student Passes the Exam}) = 0.7\n$$\nWe figured these out earlier in the lesson. Note that the probabilities\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\hat p (\\textrm{Students is from School 2}) = 0.6, \\qquad \\hat p (\\textrm{Student Passes the Exam}) = 0.3\n$$\ndo not form additional constraints because they can be derived using the rule of complements. Similarly, the probabilities of the various intersections do not form additional constraints.\n\nTherefore, the number of degrees of freedom for this test is\n\n$$\n\\nu = 4 - 3 = 1\n$$\n\nMore generally, given a contingency table with $n$ rows and $m$ columns:\n\n- The total number of observations must equal $N$, and the estimates $\\hat p (A_1), \\hat p(A_2),\\ldots \\hat p(A_{n-1})$ and $\\hat p(B_1), \\hat p(B_2), \\ldots \\hat p(B_{m-1})$ set a constraint each. The estimates $\\hat p(A_n)$ and $\\hat p(B_m)$ can be deduced by the others, so they don't create a constraint.\n\n- Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ν & =\\text{number of cells}-\\text{number of constraints} \\\\  & =nm-1-(n-1)-(m-1) \\\\  & =nm-n-m+1 \\\\  & =(n-1)(m-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186173137 :tutorial/content "Let's use our knowledge of the uniqueness property of moment-generating functions to prove some general results.\n\n- **Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables**: Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)$ are independent. Then\n$Y = X_1+X_2+ \\cdots + X_n \\sim B(n,p)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_i}(t)$ be the moment-generating function of $X_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots, n$. Then, we have\n$M_{X_i}(t) = 1 - p + pe^t$.\nBy the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions, we have that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t)\\cdot \\hspace{0.222em}⋯\\hspace{0.222em}\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{n}}(t) \\\\  & =(1-p+p{e}^{t})\\cdot (1-p+p{e}^{t})\\cdot \\hspace{0.222em}⋯\\hspace{0.222em}\\cdot (1-p+p{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =(1-p+p{e}^{t})^{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is the moment-generating function of a binomial random variable with distribution $B(n, p)$. Now, since any given distribution is characterized by its moment-generating function (meaning that no other distribution can have the same moment-generating function), we conclude that\n$Y\\sim B(n,p)$.\n- **Sums of Binomial Random Variables**: Suppose $X_1\\sim B(n_1,p)$ and $X_2\\sim B(n_2,p)$ are independent. Then\n$Y = X_1+X_2\\sim B(n_1+n_2, p)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$ be the moment-generating functions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. Then, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = (pe^t+q)^{n_1}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = (pe^t+q)^{n_2}.\n$$\nBy the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & =(p{e}^{t}+q)^{{n}_{1}}\\cdot (p{e}^{t}+q)^{{n}_{2}} \\\\  & =(p{e}^{t}+q)^{{n}_{1}+{n}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is the moment-generating function of a binomial random variable with distribution $B(n_1+n_2, p)$. Therefore, by the uniqueness property, we conclude\n$Y\\sim B(n_1+n_2, p)$.\n- **Sums of Poisson Random Variables**: If $X_1\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_1)$ and $X_2\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_2)$ are independent, then\n$Y = X_1+X_2\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$ be the moment-generating functions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. Then, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = e^{\\lambda_1(e^t-1)}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = e^{\\lambda_2(e^t-1)}.\n$$\nBy the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}({e}^{t}-1)}\\cdot {e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}({e}^{t}-1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}({e}^{t}-1)+{\\lambda }_{2}({e}^{t}-1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2})({e}^{t}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is the moment-generating function of a Poisson random variable with distribution $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$. Therefore, by the uniqueness property, we conclude\n$Y\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$.\n\n- **Sums of Normal Random Variables**: Suppose $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2)$ are independent. Then\n$Y = X_1+X_2\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$ be the moment-generating functions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. Then, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = \\exp\\left(\\mu_1 t + \\frac{1}{2} \\sigma^2_1 t^2\\right), \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = \\exp\\left(\\mu_2 t + \\frac{1}{2} \\sigma^2_2 t^2\\right),\n$$\nwhere $\\exp(x) = e^x$ is the exponential function.By the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions and by the properties of the exponential function, we have that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}({\\mu }_{1}t+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}({\\mu }_{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}({\\mu }_{1}t+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}{t}^{2}+{\\mu }_{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}(({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2})t+\\frac{1}{2}({\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}){t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is the moment-generating function of a normal random variable with distribution $N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)$. Therefore, by the uniqueness property, we conclude\n$Y\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)$.\n\n- **Sums of Chi-Square Random Variables**:\nSuppose $X_1\\sim \\chi^2(k_1)$ and $X_2\\sim \\chi^2(k_2)$ are independent. Then\n$Y = X_1+X_2\\sim \\chi^2(k_1+k_2)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$ be the moment-generating functions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. Then, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = (1-2t)^{-k_1/2}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = (1-2t)^{-k_2/2}.\n$$\nBy the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions, we have that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & =(1-2t)^{-{k}_{1}/2}\\cdot (1-2t)^{-{k}_{2}/2} \\\\  & =(1-2t)^{-({k}_{1}+{k}_{2})/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is the moment-generating function of a chi-square random variable with distribution $\\chi^2(k_1+k_2)$. Therefore, by the uniqueness property, we conclude\n$Y\\sim \\chi^2(k_1+k_2)$."] [17592186173282 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x+2\n$$\n\nLet's prove that this function is injective.\n\nFirst, we recall the definition of an injection:\n\n> *A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is an injection if every element $x\\in X$ is mapped to a distinct element in $Y{:}$*\n> $x_1 \\neq x_2 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad f(x_1) \\neq f(x_2)$\n\nThis statement contains some negations. Thus, it's easier to show that a function is injective using the (equivalent) contrapositive statement:\n\n> *This implication is equivalent to its contrapositive:*\n> $f(x_1)=f(x_2) \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad x_1=x_2$\n\nTherefore, the injectivity of $f$ means the following:\n\n> *In our case, for all $x_1,x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$,*\n> $3x_1+2 = 3x_2+2 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad x_1 = x_2$.\n\nSo, to prove the injectivity of $f$, we need to derive $x_1=x_2$ by assuming that\n\n$$\nf(x_1)=f(x_2){:}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> *Let $x_1,x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and suppose that $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$. Then, we have the following:*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2 & =3{x}_{2}+2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis proves that\n\n$$\nf(x_1) = f(x_2)\n$$\n\nimplies $x_1 = x_2$.\n\nFinally, we state the conclusion:\n\n> *Hence, $x_1=x_2$, as was to be shown. Therefore, $f$ is an injection.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate our proposition and its proof."] [17592186173285 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x+1\n$$\n\nLet's prove that this function is surjective.\n\nFirst, recall the definition of a surjection:\n\n> *A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is a surjection if, for every element $y$ in $Y$, there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y{:}$*\n> $\\forall \\, y \\in Y, \\, \\exists \\, x \\in X, \\: f(x)=y$\n\nTherefore, in this case, the surjectivity of $f$ means the following:\n\n> *For all $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$ there exists $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $3x+1=y$.*\n\nTo prove this, we need to take an arbitrary $y \\in Y$ and show that we can always find at least one value of $x\\in X$ that is mapped to $y$ under $f$.\n\n> *So, let $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then, we have the following:*\n> $\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =y \\\\ 3x+1 & =y \\\\ 3x & =y-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{y-1}{3}\\end{aligned}$\n\nSo, if we wish to map to $y\\in\\mathbb R$, we simply select\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{y-1}{3}\\in\\mathbb R\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{y-1}{3}\\right) = 3\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{y-1}{3}\\right) + 1 = y\n$$\n\nWe continue our proof as follows:\n\n> *So, for every $y\\in \\mathbb R$, we have*\n> $f\\left(\\dfrac{y-1}{3}\\right) = y$.\n\nFinally, we state the conclusion:\n\n> *Therefore, $f$ is a surjection.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate our proposition and its proof."] [17592186173289 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n> *The function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, defined by $f(x)=3x+2$, is injective.*\n\nProof:\n\n> *A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is an injection if every element $x\\in X$ is mapped to a distinct element in $Y{:}$*\n> $x_1 \\neq x_2 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad f(x_1) \\neq f(x_2)$\n> *This implication is equivalent to its contrapositive:*\n> $f(x_1)=f(x_2) \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad x_1=x_2$\n> *In our case, for all $x_1,x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$,*\n> $3x_1+2 = 3x_2+2 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad x_1 = x_2$.\n> *Let $x_1,x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and suppose that $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$. Then, we have the following:*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2 & =3{x}_{2}+2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1} & =3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> *Hence, $x_1=x_2$, as was to be shown. Therefore, $f$ is an injection.*"] [17592186173290 :tutorial/content "*Proposition:*\n\n> *The function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, defined by $f(x)=3x+1$, is surjective.*\n\n*Proof:*\n\n> *A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is a surjection if, for every element $y$ in $Y$, there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y{:}$*\n> $\\forall \\, y \\in Y, \\, \\exists \\, x \\in X, \\: f(x)=y$\n> *For all $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$ there exists $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $3x+1=y$.*\n> *So, let $y \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then, we have the following:*\n> $\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =y \\\\ 3x+1 & =y \\\\ 3x & =y-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{y-1}{3}\\end{aligned}$\n> *So, for every $y\\in \\mathbb R$, we have*\n> $f\\left(\\dfrac{y-1}{3}\\right) = y$.\n> *Therefore, $f$ is a surjection.*"] [17592186173294 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following proposition by contradiction:\n\n> $10x+4y \\neq 1$ for all $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nFirst, let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\phantom{\\lnot}P: \\quad \\forall x, y\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 10x+4y \\neq 1\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P: \\quad \\exists x, y\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 10x+4y = 1\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that there exists $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $10x+4y = 1$.*\n\nThe idea is to divide both sides by a specific number and show that the left-hand side is an integer while the right-hand side is not.\n\nSince $10$ and $4$ have a common factor of $2$, we can divide the left-hand side by this factor without introducing fractional coefficients.\n\n> *Dividing both sides by $2$ gives*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}10x+4y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{10x+4y}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{10x}{2}+\\frac{4y}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 5x+2y & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we show that we've reached a contradiction.\n\n> *Since $x$ and $y$ are integers, we have that $5x+2y$ is an integer.*\n> *But this is a contradiction since $\\dfrac12$ is not an integer.*\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our assumption that $\\lnot P$ is true is incorrect. Hence, $P$ is true, and we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> *Therefore, $10x+4y \\neq 1$ for any $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's state the full proof."] [17592186173295 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n> $10x+4y \\neq 1$ for all $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nProof:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that there exists $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $10x+4y = 1$.*\n> *Dividing both sides by $2$ gives*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}10x+4y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{10x+4y}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{10x}{2}+\\frac{4y}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 5x+2y & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> *Since $x$ and $y$ are integers, we have that $5x+2y$ is an integer.*\n> *But this is a contradiction since $\\dfrac12$ is not an integer.*\n> *Therefore, $10x+4y \\neq 1$ for any $x,y \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.*"] [17592186175752 :question/worked-solution "The moment-generating function of a continuous random variable $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nM(t) =\\textrm E \\left[ e^{tX} \\right] =\\int^{\\infty}_{-\\infty} f (x) e^{tx} \\,\\textrm dx\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is its probability density function.\n\nGiven that $X\\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(5)$, we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}5 {e}^{- 5 x} , & x \\ge  0 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(t) &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x)e^{tx}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{∞}5e^{-5x}e^{tx}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{∞}5e^{(t - 5)x}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- For $t=5$, our integral becomes\n$$\n\\displaystyle M(5) = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\infty} 5e^{(5-5)x} \\, \\textrm dx = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\infty} 5 \\, \\textrm dx,\n$$\nwhich doesn't converge.\n- For $t \\neq 5$, our integral becomes\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}5{e}^{(t-5)x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{5}{t-5}{e}^{(t-5)x}|}_{0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{5}{t-5}{e}^{(t-5)x}-\\frac{5}{t-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{t-5}\\lim_{x\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{(t-5)x}-\\frac{5}{t-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTo evaluate the limit at infinity, we consider two cases:If $t < 5$, then $t-5< 0$, and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{(t - 5)x} = 0.\n$$If $t > 5$, then $t-5 > 0$, and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} e^{(t - 5)x} = \\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nM(t) =-\\dfrac{5}{t-5} = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{5}{5-t}}, \\qquad t < 5\n$$\n\nand $M(t)$ is undefined for\n\n$$\nt \\geq 5\n$$"] [17592186175754 :question/worked-solution "The moment-generating function of a continuous random variable $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nM(t) =\\textrm E \\left[ e^{tX} \\right] =\\int^{\\infty}_{-\\infty} f (x) e^{tx}\\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is its probability density function.\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{{x} \\cdot e^{-x/2}}{4 \\cdot \\Gamma\\left(2 \\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(t) &= \\int_{- ∞}^{∞}f(x)e^{tx}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{∞}(x \\cdot e^{-x/2})/(4 \\cdot Γ(2)) \\cdot e^{tx}dx \\\\\n&= (1)/(4 \\cdot Γ(2))\\int_{0}^{∞}x \\cdot e^{tx - x/2}dx \\\\\n&= (1)/(4 \\cdot Γ(2))\\int_{0}^{∞}x \\cdot e^{x(t - 1/2)}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac 12$ our integral becomes\n$$\n\\displaystyle M\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right) = \\displaystyle \\int^{\\infty}_{0} {x} \\cdot e^{x(1/2-1/2)} \\, \\textrm dx = \\displaystyle \\int^{\\infty}_{0} {x} \\,\\textrm dx,\n$$\nwhich doesn't converge.\n- For $t > \\dfrac 12$, we have $t - \\dfrac 12 > 0$, which means\n$x e^{x\\left(t- 1/2\\right)} \\to \\infty\\quad \\textrm{as} \\quad x \\to \\infty$.\nSo, our integral doesn't converge.\n- For $t < \\dfrac 12$, we use the substitution\n$$\n\\displaystyle u = x\\left(\\dfrac 12 - t\\right) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\mathrm d x = \\dfrac{1}{1/2 - t} \\mathrm d u.\n$$\nRewriting our integral, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}x\\cdot {e}^{x(t-1/2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}\\frac{1}{(1/2-t)}\\left(\\frac{u}{1/2-t}\\right){e}^{-u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1/2-t)^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}u\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{-u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1/2-t)^{2}}\\cdot \\mathrm{Γ}(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle M ( t ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot  \\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 )}} {\\int }_{0}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }} x \\cdot  {e}^{x ( t - 1 / 2 )} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{d} x \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot  \\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 )}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( 1 / 2 - t {)}^{2}}} \\cdot  \\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{4 \\cdot  \\cancel{\\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 )}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( 1 / 2 - t {)}^{2}}} \\cdot  \\cancel{\\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( 2 )} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {2}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( 1 / 2 - t {)}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\bbox[3pt,gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{( 1 - 2 t {)}^{2}}}}} \\hspace{1em} \\text{for} \\hspace{1em} t < {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nand $M(t)$ is undefined for\n\n$$\nt\\geq \\dfrac 12\n$$"] [17592186176518 :question/problem "From the information given below, find all of the angles that are congruent.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}A & ={60}^{∘}, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}B & ={30}^{∘}, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}C & ={20}^{∘}, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}D & =m\\mathrm{∠}A, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}E & ={90}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}B, \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}F & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186176956 :tutorial/content "The table below gives the moment-generating functions $M(t)$ of some common continuous probability distributions.\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a,b]$ | ${\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right. }$ | $t\\in\\mathbb R$ |\n| $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ | $\\exp\\left({\\mu t+\\dfrac12\\sigma^2t^2}\\right)$ | $\\,\\,\\,t\\in\\mathbb R\\,\\,\\,$ |\n| $\\chi^2(k)$ | $(1-2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t<\\dfrac12$ |\n| $\\text{Exp}(\\lambda)$ | $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n| $\\Gamma(\\alpha,\\lambda)$ | $\\left(\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda-t}\\right)^\\alpha$ | $t<\\lambda$ |\n\nFrom here, we can make some observations:\n\n- At the beginning of this lesson, we proved the formula for a continuous uniform random variable $X\\sim U[a,b]$ for some specific values of $a$ and $b$, where we assumed $t\\neq 0$. However, when $t=0$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M(0) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b-a}\\cdot {e}^{0\\cdot x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{1}{b-a}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{b-a}{b-a} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Notice the similarity between the MGFs of the exponential and gamma random variables. This is reflective of the relationship between the distributions: $\\textrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ gives the total time until the next event occurs in a Poisson process, while $\\Gamma(\\alpha, \\lambda)$ gives the total time for $\\alpha$ events to occur. In a future lesson, we will discuss the precise relationship between these two MGFs."] [17592186177020 :tutorial/content "Suppose that Alice has a $20\\%$ chance of being late for work if it rains and a $5\\%$ chance of being late for work if it doesn't. Where Alice lives, the probability that it rains on a randomly selected day is $10\\%$.\n\nAlice's boss wants to determine the probability that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day.\n\nOne way of modeling this situation is to use a tree diagram.\n\nBefore we draw our tree diagram, let's summarize what we know:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{Rain}) = 0.1, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid Rain}) = 0.2, \\qquad P(\\textrm{Late \\mid No Rain}) = 0.05\n$$\n\nWe're given probability data on Alice being late *given* whether or not it rains, which suggests that we set up our tree diagram as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3af417611214953e21920827706eb266e8c069414271980120f3f70a60e95fb5.png)\n\nWe now fill in the missing probabilities:\n\n- For the missing probability on the first level of our diagram, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\text{No Rain}) & =1-P(\\text{Rain}) \\\\  & =1-0.1 \\\\  & =0.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the missing probabilities on the second level, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\text{Not Late|Rain}) & =1-P(\\text{Late|Rain}) \\\\  & =1-0.2 \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\text{Not Late|No Rain}) & =1-P(\\text{Late|No Rain}) \\\\  & =1-0.05 \\\\  & =0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these probabilities to our diagram.\n\n![](images/f2/f2a896e33e582a384d8ed3f21a1b55f6aaf96641154c55e93d83f344122613af.png)\n\nTo calculate the probability that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day, we first highlight the relevant branches.\n\n![](images/ea/ead309fdf29fcf57a8d1ab6911650ac3cdc551207a9934d9c655469121fbdfd0.png)\n\nRemember that we multiply *across* the branches and add *between* them. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( \\text{Late} ) & \\displaystyle  = P ( \\text{Rain} ) \\cdot  P ( \\text{Late|Rain} ) + P ( \\text{No Rain} ) \\cdot  P ( \\text{Late|No Rain} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 0.1 ) ( 0.2 ) + ( 0.9 ) ( 0.05 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.065 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 6.5 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a $6.5\\%$ chance that Alice will be late on a randomly selected day."] [17592186177025 :tutorial/content "A company manufactures canned mixed nuts. The price function for this company is given by\n\n$$\np(x) = 60-0.01x\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced and sold per month. Additionally, each unit costs $\\$25$ to make, and the company's fixed costs are $\\$8\\,800$ per month.\n\nLet's calculate the profit function $P(x)$ for this company.\n\nRecall that profit function $P(x)$ is equal to the revenue function $R(x)$ minus the cost function $C(x)$:\n\n$$\nP(x)=R(x)-C(x)\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate the profit function, we first need to construct $R(x)$ and $C(x)$.\n\n- We're given the price function $p(x)$, so the revenue function is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{price per unit}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x(60-0.01x) \\\\  & =60x-0.01{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- We're also given that the fixed costs are $\\$8\\,800$ per month and that each unit costs $\\$25$ to make. Therefore, the cost function is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =(\\text{fixed cost})+(\\text{number of units})\\cdot (\\text{cost per unit}) \\\\  & =8\\hspace{0.167em}800+x\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =8\\hspace{0.167em}800+25x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now calculate the profit function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= R(x) - C(x) \\\\\n&= (60x - 0.01x^{2}) - (8800 + 25x) \\\\\n&= 60x - 0.01x^{2} - 8800 - 25x \\\\\n&=-0.01x^{2} + 35x - 8800\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179425 :question/worked-solution "The town's population increases by $1.5\\%$ per year; that is, by a factor of\n\n$$\n(1+0.015)=1.015\n$$\n\nevery year.\n\nLet $t$ denote the number of years and $P(t)$ the population of the town. We can construct a table for the relationship between $t$ and $P(t)$, as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(t)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo determine if the relationship is linear or exponential, we need to determine how $P(t)$ changes as $t$ increases at each step:\n\n- For a linear relationship, there should be a common *difference* between successive values of $P(t)$.\n- For an exponential relationship, there should be a common *ratio* between successive values of $P(t)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. From our table, we see that $P(3) = 15\\,000\\cdot(1.015)^3$.\n- Statement II is false. Let's check for a linear pattern by finding the difference between successive values of $P(t){:}$\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{2} - 15000 \\cdot (1.015) & \\approx 228 \\\\ 15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{3} - 15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{2} & \\approx 232 \\\\ 15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{4} - 15000 \\cdot (1.015)^{3} & \\approx 235\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nThis list of differences does not show a definite common difference.\n- Statement III is true. Let's check for a common ratio by dividing successive values of $P(t){:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15\\hspace{0.167em}000\\cdot (1.015)^{2}}{15\\hspace{0.167em}000\\cdot (1.015)} & =1.015 \\\\ \\frac{15\\hspace{0.167em}000\\cdot (1.015)^{3}}{15\\hspace{0.167em}000\\cdot (1.015)^{2}} & =1.015 \\\\ \\frac{15\\hspace{0.167em}000\\cdot (1.015)^{4}}{15\\hspace{0.167em}000\\cdot (1.015)^{3}} & =1.015\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis list of quotients shows a definite common ratio of $1.015$.\n\nTherefore, the function that best models the relationship is an exponential function with a common ratio of $1.015$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\phantom{A}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SteelBlue}\\overset{+1}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(t)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 1.015}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 1.015}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 1.015}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{SandyBrown}\\underset{\\times 1.015}{\\longrightarrow}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186179519 :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function, we note the following:\n\n- The function has a vertical asymptote at $x = -1$. Therefore, the function's denominator must be zero at $x = -1$, but the numerator should not be zero at this point. This allows us to discard the following option:\n$\\dfrac {x^2-1}{x+1}$\n- The function has an open circle at $x = 1$. This means that $x = 1$ is a hole, and so the numerator and denominator are both zero at $x = 1$. This allows us to discard the following option:\n$y = \\dfrac{x}{x-1}$So, we're left with the following options:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\dfrac {x^2-1}{x^4-1}, \\qquad y =\\dfrac {x^2-x}{x^2-1}\n$$\n- The function has a horizontal asymptote at $y = 1$. This means that $y \\to 1$ as $x \\to \\infty$. Note that for large $\\mid x \\mid$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\approx 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, we can discard this option.\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the only one that satisfies the above conditions is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {x^2-x}{x^2-1}\n$$"] [17592186179520 :question/worked-solution "First, we denote\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{5}{x^2+4x-5}\n$$\n\nTo determine the correct graph, we follow four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** We find the $y$-intercept. To do this, we evaluate $f(0)$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{5}{(0)^{2}+4(0)-5} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{5}{-5} \\\\ f(0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the $y$-intercept is $(0,-1)$.\n- **Step 2:** We find the $x$-intercepts. To do this, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x$.In this case, the numerator is a constant, and so there are no $x$-intercepts.\n- **Step 3:** We find the vertical asymptotes. To do this, we need to set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x-5 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-5$ and $x=1$.\n- **Step 4:** We find the horizontal asymptotes. To do this, we divide numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, which is $x^2$ in this case:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}{(1+\\frac{4}{x}-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, as $\\mid x \\mid$ gets very large, $\\dfrac{5}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{4}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $\\mid x \\mid \\to\\infty$, we have\n$f(x) \\mid \\approx \\frac{0}{1 + 0 - 0} = 0$.\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0$.\n\nFinally, among the options given, only graph I satisfies the above conditions:\n\n![](images/ab/abfae9da161b5c9368d0ff2729755c7c463a0a122b7be00e7b525339b8841dd2.png)"] [17592186179536 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}\\log_{2} (x + 1) - x\\log_{2} (x + 1) - 2\\log_{2} (x + 1) &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - x - 2)\\log_{2} (x + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - x - 2 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\log_2 (x+1) =0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2- x-2 = 0$ can be factored as\n$(x+1)(x-2) = 0$,\nwhich gives the solutions $x=-1,2$.\n- The equation $\\log_2 (x+1) =0$ can be solved as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x+1 & ={2}^{0} \\\\ x+1 & =1 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have three candidate solutions, $x=-1, 0, 2$. However, we need to check whether these solutions are in the domain of the logarithm in the original equation.\n\nThe original equation contains the logarithm $\\log_2 (x+1)$, and its domain is $(-1, \\infty)$.\n\n- The candidate $x=-1$ does not lie in the domain, so we discard it.\n- The candidates $x=0,2$ lie in the domain, so we keep them.\n\nTherefore, the original equation has two solutions $x=0, 2$. The sum of these solutions is\n\n$$\n0+2=2\n$$"] [17592186179537 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{2}\\log_{3} (4x) &= 12\\log_{3} (4x) \\\\\n3x^{2}\\log_{3} (4x) - 12\\log_{3} (4x) &= 0 \\\\\n(3x^{2} - 12)\\log_{3} (4x) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3x^2 -12 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\log_3 (4x) =0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3x^2 -12=0$ can be factored as\n$3(x+2)(x-2)= 0$,\nwhich gives the solutions $x=-2,2$.\n- The equation $\\log_3 (4x) = 0$ can be solved as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}(4x) & =0 \\\\ 4x & ={3}^{0} \\\\ 4x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have three candidate solutions,\n\n$$\nx=-2,\\frac{1}{4},2\n$$\n\nHowever, we need to check whether these solutions are in the domain of the logarithm in the original equation.\n\nThe original equation contains the logarithm $\\log_3(4x)$, and its domain is $(0, \\infty)$.\n\n- The candidate $x=-2$ lies outside the domain, so we discard it.\n- The candidates $x=\\dfrac{1}{4},2$ lie within the domain, so we accept them as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the original equation has two solutions\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{4},2\n$$\n\nThe sum of these solutions is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}+2=\\dfrac{9}{4}\n$$"] [17592186179565 :question/worked-solution "Let $S_n$ be the value of Monica's investment at the end of the $n$th year.\n\n- At the end of the year $1$, Monica's investment will be worth\n$S_1 = \\$3\\,500\\cdot (1.02)$.\n- To calculate the value of the investment at the end of year $2$, we need to add $\\$3\\,500$ to the balance at the end of year $1$, and then increase the entire amount by $2 \\%$. Therefore, at the end of the year $2$, Monica's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{2}&= (S_{1} + \\$3500) \\cdot (1.02) \\\\ &= (\\$3500 \\cdot (1.02) + \\$3500) \\cdot (1.02) \\\\ &= \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02)^{2} + \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Similarly, at the end of the year $3$, Monica's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{3}&= (S_{2} + \\$3500) \\cdot (1.02) \\\\ &= [(\\$3500 \\cdot (1.02)^{2} + \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02)) + \\$3500] \\cdot (1.02) \\\\ &= \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02)^{3} + \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02)^{2} + \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- $\\quad \\vdots$\n- Continuing this process, we may deduce that by the end of the year $9$, Monica's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{9}&= \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02)^{9} + \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02)^{8} + ⋯ + \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02) \\\\ &= \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02) \\cdot [(1.02)^{8} + (1.03)^{7} + ⋯ + 1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression inside the square brackets is a geometric series with $a_1 = 1$, $r = 1.02$, and $N = 9$. Thus, after $9$ years, Monica's investment will be worth\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{9} &= \\$3500 \\cdot (1.02) \\cdot ((a_{1}(1 - r^{N}))/(1 - r)) \\\\\n&= \\$3570 \\cdot ((1 \\cdot (1 - 1.02^{9}))/(1 - 1.02)) \\\\\n&= \\$3570 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 - 1.02^{9}}{1 - 1.02}\\right) \\\\\n&\\approx \\$34824\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest dollar."] [17592186179566 :question/worked-solution "Let $S_n$ be the value of Charlie's investment at the end of the $n$th year.\n\n- The interest rate is $4.8 \\%$, compounded monthly. Therefore, at the end of year $1$, Charlie's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_1 = \\$1\\,000\\cdot \\left(1 + \\dfrac{0.048}{12}\\right)^{12} = \\$1\\,000 \\cdot \\left(1.004\\right)^{12}.\n$$\n- To calculate the value of the investment at the end of year $2$, we need to add $\\$1\\,000$ to the balance at the end of year $1$, and then increase the entire amount by $4.8 \\%$, compounded monthly. Therefore, at the end of year $2$, Charlie's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{2}&= (S_{1} + 1000) \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\\\ &= (\\$1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} + \\$1000) \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\\\ &= \\$1000 \\cdot [(1.004)^{12}]^{2} + \\$1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- $\\quad \\vdots$\n- Continuing this process, we may deduce that by the end of year $6$, Charlie's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{6}&= \\$1000 \\cdot [(1.004)^{12}]^{6} + \\$1000 \\cdot [(1.004)^{12}]^{5} + ⋯ + \\$1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\\\ &= \\$1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\cdot {[(1.004)^{12}]^{5} + [(1.004)^{12}]^{4} + ⋯ + [(1.004)^{12}] + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression inside the curly braces is a geometric series with $a_1 = 1$,\n\n$$\nr = (1.004)^{12}\n$$\n\nand $N = 6$. Thus, after $6$ years, Charlie's investment will be worth\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{6} &= \\$1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\cdot ((a_{1}(1 - r^{N}))/(1 - r)) \\\\\n&= \\$1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\cdot ((1 \\cdot (1 - [(1.004)^{12}]^{6}))/(1 - (1.004)^{12})) \\\\\n&= \\$1000 \\cdot (1.004)^{12} \\cdot ((1 - [(1.004)^{12}]^{6})/(1 - (1.004)^{12})) \\\\\n&\\approx \\$7119\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest dollar."] [17592186179567 :question/worked-solution "Let $S_n$ be the value of Harold's investment at the end of the $n$th year.\n\n- The interest rate is $4\\%$ per year, compounded continuously. Therefore, at the end of year $1$, Harold's investment will be worth\n$S_1 = \\$2\\,500e^{0.04}$\n- To calculate the value of the investment at the end of year $2$, we need to add $\\$2\\,500$ to the balance at the end of year $1$, and then increase the entire amount by $4\\%$, compounded continuously. Therefore, at the end of year $2$, Harold's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{2}&= (S_{1} + 2500)e^{0.04} \\\\ &= (\\$2500 \\cdot e^{0.04} + \\$2500) \\cdot e^{0.04} \\\\ &= \\$2500 \\cdot [e^{0.04}]^{2} + \\$2500 \\cdot e^{0.04}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- $\\quad \\vdots$\n- Continuing this process, we may deduce that by the end of year $10$, Harold's investment will be worth\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{10}&= \\$2500 \\cdot [e^{0.04}]^{10} + \\$2500 \\cdot [e^{0.04}]^{9} + ⋯ + \\$2500 \\cdot e^{0.04} \\\\ &= \\$2500 \\cdot e^{0.04} \\cdot {[e^{0.04}]^{9} + [e^{0.03}]^{8} + ⋯ + [e^{0.04}] + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression inside the curly braces is a geometric series with $a_1=1$, $r=e^{0.04}$, and $N=10$. Thus, after $10$ years, Harold's investment will be worth\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{10} &= \\$2500 \\cdot e^{0.04} \\cdot ((a_{1}(1 - r^{N}))/(1 - r)) \\\\\n&= \\$2500 \\cdot e^{0.04} \\cdot ((1 \\cdot (1 - [e^{0.04}]^{10}))/(1 - e^{0.04})) \\\\\n&= \\$2500 \\cdot e^{0.04} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 - [e^{0.04}]^{10}}{1 - e^{0.04}}\\right) \\\\\n&\\approx \\$31358\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest dollar."] [17592186179569 :question/worked-solution "From the graph of the function, we note the following:\n\n- The function has only one vertical asymptote, $x = 1$. Therefore, the function's denominator must be zero at $x=1$, but the numerator should not be zero at this point. This allows us to discard the following option:\n$y = \\dfrac{2(x^2-x)}{x^2-1}$\n- The function has two open circles, at $x = 0$ and $x=2$. This means that $x = 0$ and $x=2$ are holes, and so the numerator and denominator are both zero at $x = 0$ and $x=2$. This allows us to discard the following option:\n$y = \\dfrac{x^2-2x}{x^2-x}$So, we're left with the following options:\n$$\n\\displaystyle y = \\dfrac{x^3-2x^2}{x(x-1)(x-2)}, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{x^2-2x}{(x^2-x)(x-2)}\n$$\n- The function has a horizontal asymptote at $y = 0$. This means that $y \\to 0$ as $\\mid x \\mid \\to \\infty$. Note that for large $\\mid x \\mid$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}}{x(x-1)(x-2)} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(x-2)}{x(x-1)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{x}{x}\\right)}{(\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & \\approx 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, we can discard the following option:\n$y = \\dfrac{x^3-2x^2}{x(x-1)(x-2)}$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the only one that satisfies the above conditions is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{x^2-2x}{(x^2-x)(x-2)}\n$$"] [17592186179570 :question/worked-solution "First, we denote\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x^2 - x}{x^2 + 2x} = \\dfrac{x(x - 1)}{x(x+2)}\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator and denominator both have $x$ as a common factor. Setting this common factor equal to zero and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\nx = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a hole at $x=0$.\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the hole, we find the reduced rational function $F(x)$ and evaluate it at the $x$-coordinate of the hole.\n\nFinding the reduced rational function $F(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle F ( x ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{x ( x - 1 )}{x ( x + 2 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{x} ( x - 1 )}{\\cancel{x} ( x + 2 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{x - 1}{x + 2}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate of the hole is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(0) &= \\frac{0 - 1}{0 + 2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the hole are\n\n$$\n\\left(0,-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, to determine the correct graph, we follow four steps with the reduced rational function $F(x){:}$\n\n- **Step 1:** We find the $y$-intercept. However, since $x=0$ is a hole, there is no $y$-intercept.\n- **Step 2:** We find the $x$-intercepts. To do this, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the $x$-intercept is $(1,0)$.\n- **Step 3:** We find the vertical asymptotes. To do this, we need to set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=-2$.\n- **Step 4:** We find the horizontal asymptotes. To do this, we divide numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, which is $x$ in this case:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(1+\\frac{2}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, as $\\mid x \\mid$ gets very large, $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $\\dfrac{2}{x}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $\\mid x \\mid \\to\\infty$, we have\n$F(x) \\mid \\approx \\frac{1 - 0}{1 + 0} = 1$.\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 1$.\n\nFinally, among the options given, only graph III satisfies the above conditions:\n\n![](images/ac/ac273091191871c64a5421018bb5afd346215042834eb1063fe123e726cbb91c.png)"] [17592186179575 :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ and factor the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{4x^{2} - 4}{x^{3} - x^{2} - 9x + 9} \\\\\n&= (4(x^{2} - 1))/(x^{2}(x - 1) - 9(x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (4(x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x^{2} - 9)) \\\\\n&= (4(x - 1)(x + 1))/((x - 1)(x + 3)(x - 3))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator and denominator both have $(x-1)$ as a common factor. Setting this common factor equal to zero and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\nx - 1 = 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x= 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has a hole at $x=1$.\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the hole, we find the reduced rational function $F(x)$ and evaluate it at the $x$-coordinate of the hole.\n\nFinding the reduced rational function $F(x)$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle F ( x ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4 ( x - 1 ) ( x + 1 )}{( x - 1 ) ( x + 3 ) ( x - 3 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4 \\cancel{( x - 1 )} ( x + 1 )}{\\cancel{( x - 1 )} ( x + 3 ) ( x - 3 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{4 ( x + 1 )}{( x + 3 ) ( x - 3 )}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate of the hole is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(1) &= (4(1 + 1))/((1 + 3)(1 - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{-8} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the hole are $(1,-1)$.\n\nNow, to determine the correct graph, we follow four steps with the reduced rational function $F(x){:}$\n\n- **Step 1:** We find the $y$-intercept. To do this, we evaluate $F(0){:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\frac{4(x+1)}{(x+3)(x-3)} \\\\ F(0) & =\\frac{4(0+1)}{(0+3)(0-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the $y$-intercept is $\\left(0,-\\dfrac{4}{9}\\right)$.\n- **Step 2:** We find the $x$-intercepts. To do this, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the $x$-intercept is $(-1,0)$.\n- **Step 3:** We find the vertical asymptotes. To do this, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=3$.\n- **Step 4:** We find the horizontal asymptotes. To do this, we divide numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, which is $x^2$ in this case:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\frac{4(x+1)}{(x+3)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x+4}{{x}^{2}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{(1-\\frac{9}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, as $\\mid x \\mid$ gets very large, $\\dfrac{4}{x}$, $\\dfrac{4}{x^2}$, and $\\dfrac{9}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $\\mid x \\mid \\to\\infty$, we have\n$F(x) \\mid \\approx \\frac{0 + 0}{1 - 0} = 0$.\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0$.\n\nFinally, among the options given, only graph III satisfies the above conditions:\n\n![](images/7c/7cd8be74b94f2a8bb76d8b0078850365d6c9f83f6d52e66e21e002c1ce7bf3d7.png)"] [17592186179609 :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{\\tan 2θ}{θ} &= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}(\\left(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ}\\right))/(θ) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}(\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0$, both numerator and denominator approach $0$. So, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin 2\\theta}{\\theta} = \\dfrac{0}{0},\n$$\nwhich is an indeterminate form. Instead, let's recall the following special limit:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x} =1\n$$We rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta }{\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}2\\theta }{2\\theta }) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{sin{\\color{blue}{2\\theta }}}{{\\color{blue}{2\\theta }}}\\end{aligned}\n$$Let $x = 2\\theta$. Then, since $x\\to0$ as $\\theta \\to 0$, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2 \\cdot \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}2\\theta}{\\color{blue}2\\theta} = 2 \\cdot \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\: \\dfrac{\\sin \\color{blue}x}{\\color{blue}x} = 2 \\cdot 1 =2.\n$$\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0} \\: \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos 0} = \\dfrac11 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{θ \\to  0}\\frac{\\sin 2θ}{θ} \\cdot lim_(θ → 0)\\frac{1}{\\cos 2θ} = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$"] [17592186179624 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\n- Let's summarize the information for the first dart:The initial velocity of the first dart is $u_1=63\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. The first dart is in motion for $t_1 = 2 +4 = 6 \\, \\textrm{s}$.The acceleration due to gravity is $g=-9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Notice that $g$ is *negative* since the initial motion of the first dart acts *against* gravity. Substituting these values into the formula, we can find the displacement of the first dart:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1} & ={u}_{1}{t}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}g{{t}_{1}\\hspace{-0.167em}}^{2} \\\\  & =(63)(6)+\\frac{1}{2}(-9.8)(6)^{2} \\\\  & =201.6\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Let's now summarize the information for the second dart:The second dart is in motion for $t_2=4\\,\\textrm{s}$. The second dart covers a distance of $s_2=s_1=201.6 \\, \\textrm{m}$, the same as the first one. The acceleration due to gravity is $g=-9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$. Notice that $g$ is *negative* since the initial motion of the second dart acts *against* gravity. Substituting these values into the formula and solving for $u_2$ (the initial velocity of the second dart), we obtain the following:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{2} & ={u}_{2}{t}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}g{{t}_{2}\\hspace{-0.167em}}^{2} \\\\ (201.6) & ={u}_{2}(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(-9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\ 201.6 & =4{u}_{2}-78.4 \\\\ 280 & =4{u}_{2} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the second dart was $70 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$."] [17592186179625 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nWe need to find the height $s$ of the cliff.\n\n- Let's summarize the information for the stone:The initial velocity of the stone is $u_1 = 0\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. The acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.Let $t_1$ denote the time it took for the stone to reach the ground.Substituting these values into the formula, we can express the distance covered by the stone (i.e., the height of the cliff) as\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={u}_{1}{t}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}g{{t}_{1}\\hspace{-0.167em}}^{2} \\\\  & =(0){t}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){{t}_{1}\\hspace{-0.167em}}^{2} \\\\  & =4.9{{t}_{1}\\hspace{-0.167em}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Let's now summarize the information for the ball:The initial velocity of the ball is $u_2 = 23.52 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$. The acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.Let $t_2$ denote the time it took for the ball to reach the ground.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we can express the distance covered by the ball as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= u_{2}t_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (23.52)t_{2} + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)t_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that\n\n$$\nt_1 = t_2 + 2\n$$\n\nsince the stone is in motion $2$ seconds more than the ball, and both objects hit the ground simultaneously.\n\nTherefore, equating our two expressions for $s$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4.9t_{1}^{2} &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n4.9(t_{2} + 2)^{2} &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n4.9(t_{2}^{2} + 4t_{2} + 4) &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n4.9t_{2}^{2} + 19.6t_{2} + 19.6 &= 4.9t_{2}^{2} + 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n19.6t_{2} + 19.6 &= 23.52t_{2} \\\\\n19.6 &= 3.92t_{2} \\\\\nt_{2} &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt_1 = 5 + 2 = 7 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nFinally, by substituting\n\n$$\nt_1=7\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\ninto the first equation for $s$, we find that the height of the cliff is\n\n$$\ns = 4.9(7)^2 = 240.1 \\, \\textrm{m}\n$$"] [17592186180170 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to  π/3}(\\frac{π}{3} - x)\\csc (x - \\frac{π}{3}).\n$$"] [17592186180695 :tutorial/content "Now let's suppose that the investor pays $\\$5\\,000$ *per year* into an investment account that generates a return of $8\\%$, compounded annually. Let $S_n$ be the value of the investment at the end of the $n$th year. Our goal is to calculate $S_{30}$, the value of the portfolio when the investor retires in $30$ years.\n\nLet's work out $S_n$ for\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3,\\ldots\n$$\n\nand see if we can spot a pattern.\n\n- At the end of year $1$, the investment will be worth\n$S_1 = \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)$.\n- To calculate the value of the investment at the end of year $2$, we first need to add $\\$5\\,000$ to the balance at the end of year $1$. This gives the expression\n$S_1 + 5\\,000$.\nThen, we increase the amount by $8 \\%$. Therefore, at the end of the year $2$, the investment will be worth\n$S_2 = \\big(S_1 + 5\\,000\\big) \\cdot (1.08)$.\nSubstituting our expression for $S_1$ and expanding the parentheses, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{2}&= (\\$5000 \\cdot (1.08) + \\$5000) \\cdot (1.08) \\\\ &= \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{2} + \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Similarly, at the end of the year $3$, the investment will be worth\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S_{3}&= (S_{2} + \\$5000) \\cdot (1.08) \\\\ &= [(\\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{2} + \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)) + \\$5000] \\cdot (1.08) \\\\ &= \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{3} + \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{2} + \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou can probably spot the pattern now. We can deduce that the expression for $S_{30}$ is equal to\n\n$$\nS_{30} = \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^{30} + \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^{29} + \\cdots + {5 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\cdot  ( 1.08 {)}^{2} +}5\\,000\\cdot (1.08).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this sum, we first factor it as follows:\n\n$$\nS_{30} = \\$5\\,000\\cdot (1.08) \\cdot \\left[ (1.08)^{29} +(1.08)^{28} + \\cdots + (1.08) + 1 \\right]\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression inside the square brackets is a geometric series with $a_1 = 1$, $r = 1.08$, and $N = 30$. Thus, after $30$ years, the investment will be worth\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{30} &= \\$5000 \\cdot (1.08) \\cdot ((a_{1}(1 - r^{N}))/(1 - r)) \\\\\n&= \\$5400 \\cdot ((1 \\cdot (1 - 1.08^{30}))/(1 - 1.08)) \\\\\n&= \\$5400 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1 - 1.08^{30}}{1 - 1.08}\\right) \\\\\n&\\approx \\$611729\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest dollar.\n\nThis example illustrates how compound interest can be used to fund retirements. The investor deposited a total of\n\n$$\n\\$5\\,000\\cdot 30 = \\$150\\,000\n$$\n\nover $30$ years, yet this was worth $\\$611\\,729$ by the time they retired!"] [17592186180746 :tutorial/content "Suppose there is an exponential relationship of the form\n\n$$\ny \\approx a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nbetween the variables $x$ and $y$, shown in the semi-log scatter plot below.\n\n![](images/4a/4a5d102e232c85c6dab65a8f09ceaf67fab255d1a00ec3bc0af6b218f2c2d94c.png)\n\nSuppose further that the semi-log scatter plot has the trend line\n\n$$\nY = 4 + 0.3x\n$$\n\nwhere $Y = \\log y$. Using only this information, it's possible to figure out the values of $a$ and $b$ in the exponential relationship\n\n$$\ny \\approx a \\cdot b^x\n$$\n\nThe first step is to transform the exponential relationship into a linear form. We can do this by taking the logarithm of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a \\cdot b^{x} \\\\\n\\log y &= \\log (a \\cdot b^{x}) \\\\\n\\log y &= \\log a + \\log b^{x} \\\\\n\\log y &= \\log a + x \\cdot \\log b \\\\\nY &= A + Bx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nY=\\log y,\\qquad A=\\log a,\\qquad B=\\log b\n$$\n\nNow, comparing\n\n$$\nY = A + Bx\n$$\n\nwith the equation\n\n$$\nY = 4 + 0.3x\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nA = 4, \\qquad B = 0.3\n$$\n\nFinally, all that's left is to substitute the values above in the equations relating $A$ to $a$ and $B$ to $b$.\n\n- Substituting $A=4$ into the equation relating $A$ to $a$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}a & =A \\\\ \\operatorname{log}a & =4 \\\\ a & ={10}^{4} \\\\ a & =10\\hspace{0.167em}000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Substituting $B=0.3$ into the equation relating $B$ to $b$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}b & =B \\\\ \\operatorname{log}b & =0.3 \\\\ b & ={10}^{0.3} \\\\ b & \\approx 2.00,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the approximate relationship between $x$ and $y$ is given by\n\n$$\ny\\approx 10\\,000 \\cdot 2^x\n$$"] [17592186183913 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that $f(x)$ is a function such that\n\n- $f(n)=a_n$ for all $n \\geq 1$,\n- $f(x)$ is continuous for $x \\geq 1$, and\n- $f(x)$ is differentiable for $x > 1$.\n\nThen,\n\n- if $f'(x) \\geq 0$ for $x \\gt 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $f'(x) \\leq 0$ for $x \\gt 1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n- $f(x) = \\dfrac{x}{(x+1)^2}$ is continuous for all $x\\geq 1$, and\n- $f(n) = a_n$ for all $n\\geq 1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking the derivative, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={\\left(\\frac{x}{(x+1)^{2}}\\right)}^{'} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1)}{(x+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{(x+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNotice that $f'(x)$ is defined for $x > 1$, so $f(x)$ is differentiable on $x \\gt 1$.Now, solving $f'(x) \\geq 0$, we get\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac {1-x^2} {(x + 1)^4} \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad -1 < x \\leq 1.\n$$\nTherefore,$f'(x) \\geq 0$ for $-1 < x \\leq 1$.$f'(x) \\leq 0$ for $x < -1$ or $x \\geq 1$.We summarize our results in the diagram below.![](images/72/723f5d6b9c691e8ce5f0e5dda6db433b52b457e893686e6ebc835ed7c4897388.png)\n- Statement II is false. Since $f'(x) \\ngeq 0$ for all $x > 1$, the sequence $a_n$ is *not* increasing.\n- Statement III is true. Since $f'(x) \\leq 0$ for all $x > 1$, the sequence $a_n$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186183915 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that $f(x)$ is a function such that\n\n- $f(n)=a_n$ for all $n \\geq 1$,\n- $f(x)$ is continuous for $x \\geq 1$, and\n- $f(x)$ is differentiable for $x > 1$.\n\nThen,\n\n- if $f'(x) \\geq 0$ for $x \\gt 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $f'(x) \\leq 0$ for $x \\gt 1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n- $f(x) = 2 + 3x - x^2$ is continuous for all $x\\geq 1$, and\n- $f(n) = a_n$ for all $n\\geq 1$.\n\nNow, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking the derivative, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={[2+3x-{x}^{2}]}^{'} \\\\  & =3-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNotice that $f'(x)$ is defined for $x > 1$, so $f(x)$ is differentiable on $x \\gt 1$.Now, solving $f'(x) \\leq 0$, we get\n$3 - 2x\\leq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x\\geq \\dfrac32$.\nTherefore, $f'(x) \\leq 0$ for $x \\geq \\dfrac32$, and $f'(x) \\geq 0$ for $x \\leq \\dfrac32$.We summarize our results in the diagram below.![](images/f5/f58e7ad6715eaf4df466a8e85f7010e3566033bb63e0e290ac870073e3a2cfb1.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f'(x) \\ngeq 0$ for all $x > 1$, the sequence $a_n$ is *not* increasing.\n- Statement III is false. Since $f'(x) \\nleq 0$ for all $x > 1$, the sequence $a_n$ is *not* decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186183965 :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be Boolean functions of three variables defined as\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}) & =max\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\}, \\\\ g({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}) & =({x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3})^{{x}_{1}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nComplete the following table corresponding to the function $g(x_1,f(x_1,x_2,x_3),x_3)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x_1,f(x_1,x_2,x_3),x_3)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186183976 :question/worked-solution "A Boolean function (logical operation) is truth-preserving if it returns $1$ (true) when all its variables are assigned a value of $1$ (true).\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- Function I is *not* truth-preserving since substituting $x_1=1$ does not give a value of $1{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =min\\{{1}^{2},\\overset{―}{1}\\} \\\\  & =min\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Function II is truth-preserving since substituting $x_1=x_2=1$ gives a value of $1{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1,1) & =1\\cdot 1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Function III is *not* truth-preserving. Indeed, substituting $x_1=x_2=x_3=1$ does not give a value of $1{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1,1,1) & =(1\\Rightarrow \\overset{―}{1})∧1 \\\\  & =(1\\Rightarrow 0)∧1 \\\\  & =0∧1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186183977 :question/worked-solution "A Boolean function (logical operation) is falsity-preserving if it returns $0$ (false) when all its variables are assigned a value of $0$ (false).\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- Function I is *not* falsity-preserving since substituting $x_1=0$ does not give a value of $0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =max\\{0,\\overset{―}{0}\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Function II is falsity-preserving since substituting $x_1=x_2=0$ gives a value of $0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0,0) & =\\overset{―}{0\\Rightarrow 0} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Function III is falsity-preserving. Indeed, substituting $x_1=x_2=x_3=0$ gives a value of $0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}) & =min\\{0,0\\}∨0 \\\\  & =0∨0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186184009 :question/worked-solution "![](images/ac/ac437252c84794f24582c2f4259ae3ecd78d3f5a94716a1c83ef3421c146b813.png)\n\nLet's apply the extended transition function to our strings.\n\n- Consider the string $011{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,011) & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (p,0)}\\delta (s,11) \\\\  & =\\delta (p,11) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (p,1)}\\delta (s,1) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,1) \\\\  & =\\delta (r)\\cup \\delta (p) \\\\  & =\\{r,p\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/e2/e21b0264a4d53dbf098f5c10144fff0c0576d30ff347db4b24e12e80ba19f5b3.png)\nThe process is terminated at the states $r$ and $p$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $011$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{accepted}}$.\n- Consider the string $0100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,0100) & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (p,0)}\\delta (s,100) \\\\  & =\\delta (p,100) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (p,1)}\\delta (s,00) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,00) \\\\  & =\\mathrm{∅}\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/e1/e17ac8432705ced6c2a16acb03896b53bfe8696e351994149d9f76845d2114b3.png)\nThe process is \"stuck\". So, the string $0100$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{rejected}}$.\n- Consider the string $1101{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,1101) & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (p,1)}\\delta (s,101) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,101) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,01)\\cup \\delta (p,01) \\\\  & =({⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (r,0)}\\delta (s,1))\\cup ({⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (p,0)}\\delta (s,0)) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,1)\\cup \\delta (p,1) \\\\  & =\\mathrm{∅}\\cup \\{q\\} \\\\  & =\\{q\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/2d/2d3acedf986e2371fbfc63295c686225e82fa91aebaefd1aa659b3dd1d7a1630.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $q$. Since $q$ is not a final (accepting) state, the string $1101$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{rejected}}$."] [17592186184082 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that $f(x)$ is a function such that\n\n- $f(n)=a_n$ for all $n \\geq 1$,\n- $f(x)$ is continuous for $x \\geq 1$, and\n- $f(x)$ is differentiable for $x > 1$.\n\nThen,\n\n- if $f'(x) \\geq 0$ for $x \\gt 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $f'(x) \\leq 0$ for $x \\gt 1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n- $f(x) = x^3 - 27x+9$ is continuous for all $x\\geq 1$,\n- $f(n) = a_n$ for all $n\\geq 1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking the derivative, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={({x}^{3}-27x+9)}^{'} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-27 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNotice that $f'(x)$ is defined for $x > 1$, so $f(x)$ is differentiable on $x \\gt 1$.Now, solving $f'(x) \\geq 0$, we get\n$3(x^2-9) \\geq 0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x \\leq -3 \\textrm{or} x \\geq 3$.\nTherefore, $f'(x) \\geq 0$ for $x \\leq -3$ or $x \\geq 3$, $f'(x) \\leq 0$ for $-3 \\leq x \\leq 3$. We summarize our results in the diagram below.![](images/8b/8b476b8581d4d67941eb46f12721dec80b2f1bb64196d3a4e03c84c27d613a96.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f'(x) \\ngeq 0$ for all $x > 1$, the sequence $a_n$ is *not* increasing.\n- Statement III is false. Since $f'(x) \\nleq 0$ for all $x > 1$, the sequence $a_n$ is *not* decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186184122 :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $\\sin 860^\\circ$ in terms of $\\sin\\theta_R$, where $\\theta_R$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle\n\n$$\n\\theta = 860^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo express $\\sin 860^\\circ$ in terms of $\\sin\\theta_R$, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find an angle that's coterminal with $860^\\circ$ that lies in the range $[0, 360^\\circ)$. We do this by subtracting integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get our desired angle:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{860}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={500}^{∘} \\\\ {860}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘} & ={140}^{∘}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore,\n$\\sin 860^\\circ = \\sin 140^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/8c/8c3331f0b9d99cc20d68ee87aa473cfa5ec27b64a12b446ad90d128a90a4051c.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta = 140^\\circ$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\theta_R = 180^\\circ - 140^\\circ= 40^\\circ.\n$$\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The sine function is positive $({\\color{blue}{+}})$ in the second quadrant. Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sin (860^\\circ) = {\\color{blue}{+}}\\sin\\theta_R = {\\color{blue}{+}}\\sin{40^\\circ}.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin 860^\\circ = \\sin 40^\\circ\n$$"] [17592186184123 :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $\\cos 970^\\circ$ in terms of $\\cos \\theta_R$, where $\\theta_R$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle\n\n$$\n\\theta = 970^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo express $\\cos 970^\\circ$ in terms of $\\cos\\theta_R$, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the corresponding coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$. We do this by subtracting integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get our desired angle.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{970}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={610}^{∘} \\\\ {970}^{∘}-2\\cdot {360}^{∘} & ={250}^{∘}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore,\n$\\cos 970^\\circ = \\cos 250^\\circ$.\n\n![](images/33/331d0ec27c253f2c1712365d5519655555377433b82b8a60c4f736eb56dbd8b0.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta = 250^\\circ$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle is\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 250^\\circ - 180^\\circ= 70^\\circ\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The cosine function is negative $({\\color{red}{-}})$ in the third quadrant. Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\cos 970^\\circ = {\\color{red}{-}}\\cos\\theta_R = {\\color{red}{-}}\\cos 70^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos 970^\\circ = -\\cos 70^\\circ\n$$"] [17592186184128 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the logarithms share the same base.\n\nFirst, we apply the power rule to the *first* term to make the coefficient in front of each logarithm the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\log_{3} (\\sqrt{2}x) - \\log_{3} (x - 1) &= \\log_{3} (3x) \\\\\n\\log_{3} (\\sqrt{2}x)^{2} - \\log_{3} (x - 1) &= \\log_{3} (3x) \\\\\n\\log_{3} (2x^{2}) - \\log_{3} (x - 1) &= \\log_{3} (3x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we combine the logarithms on the left-hand side using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} \\left(\\frac{2x^{2}}{x - 1}\\right) &= \\log_{3} (3x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the equation for $x$ using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 {x}^{2}}{x - 1}} & \\displaystyle  = 3 x \\\\ \\displaystyle ( x - 1 ) \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 {x}^{2}}{x - 1}} & \\displaystyle  = ( x - 1 ) \\cdot  ( 3 x ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{( x - 1 )} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{2 {x}^{2}}{\\cancel{x - 1}}} & \\displaystyle  = ( x - 1 ) \\cdot  ( 3 x ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 {x}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 3 {x}^{2} - 3 x \\\\ \\displaystyle {x}^{2} - 3 x & \\displaystyle  = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle x ( x - 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = 0\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, $x = 0$ and $x =3$.\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting them back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 0)-{log}_{3}(0-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(3\\cdot 0) \\\\ 2{log}_{3}(0)-{log}_{3}(-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(0).\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis statement is false because $\\log_3 (0)$ and $\\log_3 (-1)$ are undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=3$ back into the original equation, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{3}(3\\sqrt{2})-{log}_{3}(3-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\ {log}_{3}{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{log}_{3}(2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(9) \\\\ {log}_{3}(18)-{log}_{3}(2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(9) \\\\ {log}_{3}\\left(\\frac{18}{2}\\right) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{3}(9) \\\\ {log}_{3}(9) & ={log}_{3}(9).\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $x=3$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=3$ only\"."] [17592186184519 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,-1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=\n$$"] [17592186185275 :tutorial/content "A nondeterministic finite automaton accepts a finite string if, after processing the entire string, the automation ends in one of its final states. Otherwise, the string is rejected. The set of all accepted finite strings is the **language** of the NFA.\n\nFor example, consider the three strings $100$, $0101$, and $0110$. Are any of these accepted by the nondeterministic automaton represented by the diagram below?\n\n![](images/e0/e057a1ecaad653f3d39e5fed6e71f95813da7d75f17e7a9e0f5529b1cf99dbfc.png)\n\nTo find out, let's apply the extended transition function to our strings.\n\n- Processing the string $100$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (q,100) & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (q,1)}\\delta (s,00) \\\\  & =\\mathrm{∅}\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/5e/5eda3e914d7d967c036c89ab6ee47f5c702a1a5b38fae45740971853c3794af5.png)\nThe process is \"stuck\". So, the string $100$ is rejected.\n\n- Processing the string $0101$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (q,0101) & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (q,0)}\\delta (s,101) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,101) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (r,1)}\\delta (s,01) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,01) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (r,0)}\\delta (s,1) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,1)\\cup \\delta (r,1) \\\\  & =\\mathrm{∅}\\cup \\{r\\} \\\\  & =\\{r\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/55/55a552f32aa9838da2a8e6b1751c8a7635d98f8457627594eaeeeeca451bc73d.png)\nThe process terminates at the state $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0101$ is accepted.\n\n- Processing the string $0110$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (q,0110) & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (q,0)}\\delta (s,110) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,110) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (r,1)}\\delta (s,10) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,10) \\\\  & ={⋃}_{s\\in \\delta (r,1)}\\delta (s,0) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,0) \\\\  & =\\{q,r\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/a8/a8d2550901c202cd4699381ccb3f642fd7daf5efb84ba36cc5e1dc3b63576508.png)\nThe process is terminated at the states $q$ and $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0110$ is accepted.\n\nThe language of this NFA consists of all finite strings of $0$'s and $1$'s that start with $0$.\n\nWe begin processing a string in state $q$. Notice that when the automaton initially reads a $1$, the automaton gets stuck. However, when it initially encounters a $0$, it switches to the accepting state $r$. If the automaton is in $r$ and processes another symbol, it remains in the accepting state.\n\nTherefore, the automaton will end in the accepting state $r$ if and only if the input starts with $0$."] [17592186185413 :tutorial/content "In formal language theory, we often construct new languages by applying operations to existing ones.\nThese operations allow us to build complex languages from simpler ones.\n\nLet $L$ and $M$ be two languages. Then, we define the following operations on languages:\n\n- The **union** $L \\cup M$ consists of all the strings that belong to either language:\n$L \\cup M = \\big\\{\\alpha: \\: \\alpha \\in L \\: \\lor \\alpha \\in M \\big\\}$\n- The **concatenation** $LM$ consists of all the strings that can be obtained by concatenating (i.e., joining) a string from the first language with a string from the second language (in this exact order):\n$LM = \\big\\{\\alpha \\beta \\:: \\: \\alpha \\in L, \\: \\beta \\in M \\big\\}$\n- The **Kleene closure** (or **Kleene star**) $L^\\ast$ consists of all the strings that can be obtained by concatenating an arbitrary (finite) number of strings from the language:\n$$\n\\displaystyle L^\\ast = \\big\\{\\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\ldots\\alpha_n \\:: \\: n \\geq 0, \\: \\alpha_i \\in L \\: \\text{for} \\: i =1,\\ldots,n \\big\\}\n$$\nNote that the empty string $\\epsilon$ also belongs to $L^\\ast$.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following languages:\n\n$$\nL = \\big\\{11, a, ab \\big\\}, \\qquad M = \\big\\{11, 10, 00 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFrom these two, we construct new languages as follows:\n\n- The union of $L$ and $M$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\cup M & =\\{\\alpha \\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\alpha \\in \\{11,a,ab\\},\\hspace{0.222em}\\alpha \\in \\{11,10,00\\}\\} \\\\  & =\\{a,\\hspace{0.222em}ab,\\hspace{0.222em}11,\\hspace{0.222em}10,\\hspace{0.222em}00\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The concatenation of $L$ and $M$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =\\{\\alpha \\beta \\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\alpha \\in \\{{\\color{blue}{11}},{\\color{blue}{a}},{\\color{blue}{ab}}\\},\\hspace{0.222em}\\beta \\in \\{{\\color{red}{11}},{\\color{red}{10}},{\\color{red}{00}}\\}\\} \\\\  & =\\{{\\color{blue}{11}}{\\color{red}{11}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{11}}{\\color{red}{10}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{11}}{\\color{red}{00}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{a}}{\\color{red}{11}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{a}}{\\color{red}{10}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{a}}{\\color{red}{00}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{ab}}{\\color{red}{11}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{ab}}{\\color{red}{10}},\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}{ab}}{\\color{red}{00}}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The Kleene closure of $L$ contains strings such as $1111$, $11ab$ and $aaba11$. Indeed, notice that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1111 & =11\\cdot 11, \\\\ 11ab & =11\\cdot ab, \\\\ aaba11 & =a\\cdot ab\\cdot a\\cdot 11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere each individual component belongs to $L$. On the other hand, some strings *not* in the closure $L^\\ast$ are$a1a$, since $1$'s can only appear in multiples of two (from the string $11 \\in L$), or $11ba$, since $b$ must be proceeded by an $a$ (from the string $ab \\in L$)."] [17592186187997 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $a_n$ is a positive sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $0 < L \\leq 1$ for all $n \\geq1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{6^n}{(n+4)!}\n$$\n\nis positive for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{n+1}}{(n+5)!}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{(n+4)!}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{n+1}\\cdot (n+4)!}{{6}^{n}\\cdot (n+5)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{n}\\cdot 6\\cdot (n+4)!}{{6}^{n}\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{6}^{n}}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(n+4)!}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{6}^{n}}\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(n+4)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n+5} \\\\  & \\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0\\lt L\\leq 1$ for all $n\\geq 1$, we can conclude that the sequence $a_n$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186187998 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $a_n$ is a positive sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $0 < L \\leq 1$ for all $n \\geq1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3}{2n^2}\n$$\n\nis positive for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2(n+1)^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2{n}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{n}^{2}}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, since $n \\geq 1$, it follows that\n$\\dfrac{n}{n + 1} \\lt 1$,\nsince the numerator is always smaller than the denominator. Therefore\n$L = \\left(\\dfrac{n}{n + 1}\\right)^2 \\lt 1$.\nSince the sequence is positive for all $n$, we can conclude that\n$0 \\lt L \\lt 1$.\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0\\lt L \\leq 1$ for all $n\\geq 1$, we can conclude that the sequence $a_n$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186188019 :question/worked-solution "First, let's convert our improper fractions to mixed numbers:\n\n- Converting $\\dfrac74$ to a mixed number, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{{\\color{blue}{4}}} & =7÷{\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{R}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{3}} \\\\  & =1\\frac{{\\color{red}{3}}}{{\\color{blue}{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Converting $\\dfrac85$ to a mixed number, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{\\color{blue}{5}}} & =8÷{\\color{blue}{5}} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{R}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{3}} \\\\  & =1\\frac{{\\color{red}{3}}}{{\\color{blue}{5}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the problem statement is now as follows:\n\n$$\n1\\dfrac34 \\,\\,\\square \\,\\,1\\dfrac35\n$$\n\nSince the whole number part is the same for both fractions, we only need to compare the fractional parts.\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34\n$$\n\nis greater than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac35\n$$\n\n(the numerators are the same, and quarters are larger than fifths), we have that\n\n$$\n1\\dfrac34 \\,\\, > \\,\\,1\\dfrac35\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac74 > \\dfrac85\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186188061 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing down the important information:\n\n- There are $8$ toy blocks.\n- Each toy block weighs $42\\,\\textrm{g}$.\n- The weight of the box is $214\\,\\textrm{g}$.\n\nLet $W$ represent the total weight of the box with the blocks inside. This is the number we want to find.\n\n- There are $8$ toy blocks, and each toy block weighs $42\\,\\textrm{g}$. Therefore, the total weight of the blocks (in grams) is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}42\\times 8 & =40\\times 8+2\\times 8 \\\\  & =320+16 \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{336}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The total weight, $W$, equals the weight calculated above $({\\color{blue} 336}\\,\\textrm{g})$ plus the weight of the box $({\\color{red}214}\\,\\textrm{g})$. Therefore, the total weight of the box with the blocks inside (in grams) is\n$W = {\\color{blue} 336} + {\\color{red} 214}$.\n\nCalculating the final sum, we get\n\n$$\nW = 336 + 214 = 550\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total weight of the box with the blocks inside is $550\\,\\textrm{g}$."] [17592186188062 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing down the important information:\n\n- Initially, there are $9$ vials, and each vial contains $21\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution.\n- Each of the $8$ students uses $10\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution.\n\nLet $A$ represent the total amount of acid solution left after the experiment. This is the number we want to find.\n\n- Initially, there are $21\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution in each of the $9$ vials. Therefore, the initial amount (in milliliters) of the acid solution is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21\\times 9 & =20\\times 9+1\\times 9 \\\\  & =180+9 \\\\  & ={\\color{red}{189}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Each of the $8$ students uses $10\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution. Therefore, the amount (in milliliters) of acid solution used during the experiment is\n$\\begin{aligned}8\\times 10 & ={\\color{blue}{80}}.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nTo find the total amount of acid solution left after the experiment, we subtract our two values:\n\n$$\nA = {\\color{red}{189}} - {\\color{blue}{80}} = 109\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $109\\,\\text{ml}$ of acid solution left after the experiment."] [17592186188079 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing down the important information:\n\n- On Monday, Nicholas ate $3$ donuts.\n- On Tuesday, he ate $4$ more donuts than on Monday.\n- Each donut weighs $28\\,\\textrm{g}$.\n\nLet $W$ be the weight of the donuts Nicholas ate on Tuesday, which is the number we want to find.\n\n- The number of donuts Nicholas ate on Tuesday is $4$ *more* than the number of donuts he ate on Monday $(3)$. Therefore, the number of donuts Nicholas ate on Tuesday is\n$4 + 3 = {\\color{blue}7}$.\n- Nicholas ate ${\\color{blue}7}$ donuts on Tuesday, and each donut weighs $28\\,\\textrm{g}$. Therefore, the weight of the donuts Nicholas ate on Tuesday (in grams) is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =28\\times {\\color{blue}{7}} \\\\  & =20\\times {\\color{blue}{7}}+8\\times {\\color{blue}{7}} \\\\  & =140+56 \\\\  & =196.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on Tuesday, Nicholas ate $196\\,\\textrm{g}$ of donuts."] [17592186188206 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $a_n$ is a positive sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $0 < L \\leq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, $a_n = 3^{n}$ is positive for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} & =\\frac{{3}^{n+1}}{{3}^{n}} \\\\  & ={3}^{n+1-n} \\\\  & ={3}^{1} \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since $L \\geq 1$ for all $n \\geq 1$, we can conclude that the sequence $a_n$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186192396 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing down the important information:\n\n- There are $24$ packs of soda.\n- Each pack contains $6$ soda cans.\n- The fridge has $8$ shelves.\n\nLet $S$ represent the total number of cans the clerk will put on each shelf. This is the number we want to find.\n\nNow, we can deduce the following:\n\n- There are $24$ packs of soda, and each pack contains $6$ cans. Therefore, the total number of soda cans is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24\\times 6 & =20\\times 6+4\\times 6 \\\\  & =120+24 \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{144}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The total number of cans $({\\color{blue}144})$ is divided equally among $8$ shelves. Therefore, the total number of cans of soda on each shelf is\n$S = {\\color{blue}144} \\div 8$.\n\nCalculating the final quotient, we get\n\n$$\nS = {\\color{blue}144} \\div 8 = 18{:}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the clerk will put $18$ cans of soda on each shelf."] [17592186192410 :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\ng(x) = x\\cdot f(x) > 0\n$$\n\nNow, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have $f'(x) \\gt 0$.\n- Statement II is false. Differentiating $g(x) = x \\cdot f(x)$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{'}(x) & =(x)^{'}\\cdot f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =f(x)+x\\cdot {f}^{'}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, since $f(x)$ is negative and strictly increasing over the interval $(-2,-1)$, we have\n$f(x) \\lt 0, \\qquad x \\lt 0, \\qquad f'(x) \\gt 0$.\nHence, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle g'(x) = \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\lt 0} + \\underbrace{x}_{< 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} = \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\lt 0} + \\underbrace{x \\cdot f'(x)}_{\\lt 0} \\lt 0.\n$$\n- Statement III is false. Since $g'(x) \\lt 0$, the function $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing. Therefore, the left Riemann sum gives an *overerestimate* of the area under the curve $y = x \\cdot f(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186192449 :question/worked-solution "We start by isolating the absolute value on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid 2x + 5 \\mid - 4x &= 1 \\\\\n\\mid 2x + 5 \\mid &= 4x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have two cases:\n\n- If $\\mid 2x + 5|=2x+ 5$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+5| & =4x+1 \\\\ 2x+5 & =4x+1 \\\\ 5 & =2x+1 \\\\ 4 & =2x \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+5| & =4x+1 \\\\ |2(2)+5| & =4(2)+1 \\\\ |4+5| & =8+1 \\\\ |9| & =9 \\\\ 9 & =9\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=2$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid 2x+5|=-(2x+5)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+5| & =4x+1 \\\\ -(2x+5) & =4x+1 \\\\ -2x-5 & =4x+1 \\\\ -5 & =6x+1 \\\\ -6 & =6x \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+5| & =4x+1 \\\\ |2(-1)+5| & =4(-1)+1 \\\\ |-2+5| & =-4+1 \\\\ |3| & =-3 \\\\ 3 & =-3\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution of our equation is $x = 2$."] [17592186192452 :question/worked-solution "We have the following two cases:\n\n- If $\\mid 2x-3|=2x-3$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-3| & =-x+1 \\\\ 2x-3 & =-x+1 \\\\ 2x & =-x+4 \\\\ 3x & =4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-3| & =-x+1 \\\\ |2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-3| & =-\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+1 \\\\ |\\frac{8}{3}-3| & =-\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\ |\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{9}{3}| & =-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{3}{3} \\\\ |-\\frac{1}{3}| & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=-\\dfrac 43$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid 2x-3|=-(2x-3)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-3| & =-x+1 \\\\ -(2x-3) & =-x+1 \\\\ -2x+3 & =-x+1 \\\\ 3 & =x+1 \\\\ 2 & =x \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-3| & =-x+1 \\\\ |2(2)-3| & =-(2)+1 \\\\ |4-3| & =-2+1 \\\\ |1| & =-1 \\\\ 1 & =-1\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, our equation has no solutions."] [17592186192457 :question/worked-solution "The $x$-coordinates of the intersection points correspond to solutions of the equation\n\n$$\n\\mid 2x+1 \\mid = -3x+11\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- For the branch with a negative slope, where $\\mid 2x+1 \\mid = -(2x+1)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+1| & =-3x+11 \\\\ -(2x+1) & =-3x+11 \\\\ -2x-1 & =-3x+11 \\\\ x-1 & =11 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+1| & =-3x+11 \\\\ |2(12)+1| & =-3(12)+11 \\\\ |24+1| & =-36+11 \\\\ |25| & =-25 \\\\ 25 & =-25\\hspace{1em}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=12$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it is not the $x$-coordinate of a point of intersection.\n\n- For the branch with a positive slope, where $\\mid 2x+1 \\mid = 2x+1$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+1| & =-3x+11 \\\\ 2x+1 & =-3x+11 \\\\ 5x+1 & =11 \\\\ 5x & =10 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x+1| & =-3x+11 \\\\ |2(2)+1| & =-3(2)+11 \\\\ |4+1| & =-6+11 \\\\ |5| & =5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=2$ satisfies the original equation, it is an $x$-coordinate of a point of intersection.\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection is $x=2$.\n\nFinally, we can find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point by substituting our solution back into either of the curves. Let's use the line\n\n$$\ny=-3x+11\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -3x + 11 \\\\\n&=-3(2) + 11 \\\\\n&=-6 + 11 \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection has coordinates $(2,5)$.\n\n![](images/dd/dda26d77c35d4cfb7250f867e16a4bec371d43878324940b8d4aa289eb0b2b72.png)"] [17592186192466 :question/worked-solution "The $x$-coordinate of $A$ corresponds to a solution of the equation\n\n$$\n-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -2x+6\n$$\n\nThe absolute value graph\n\n$$\ny=-\\mid x-2 \\mid\n$$\n\nhas two branches:\n\n- where $-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -(x-2)$, which has a negative slope, and\n- where $-\\mid x-2|=x-2$, which has a positive slope.\n\nNow, let's consider each branch in turn:\n\n- No points of intersection lie on the branch with a positive slope, so we can ignore this branch.\n- The point $A$ lies on the branch with a negative slope. So, if $-\\mid x-2 \\mid = -(x-2)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x-2) & =-2x+6 \\\\ -x+2 & =-2x+6 \\\\ x+2 & =6 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-|x-2| & =-2x+6 \\\\ -|4-2| & =-2(4)+6 \\\\ -|2| & =-8+6 \\\\ -|2| & =-2 \\\\ -2 & =-2\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=4$ satisfies the original equation, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $x=4$.\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $4$."] [17592186192497 :question/worked-solution "To convert $168$ inches into yards, we convert $168$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $168$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.\nNow, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:\n$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$\nWe want to determine how many feet are in $168$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation $168{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}168\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} & =168\\times 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{in} \\\\ \\frac{168}{12}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} & =168\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNow, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{168}{12}$ as the division $168 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:\n$1$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$\nTherefore, $168\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n\n- Next, to convert from $14$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.\nNow, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:\n$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$\nWe want to determine how many yards are in $14$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation $17{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\times \\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd} & =14\\times 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} \\\\ \\frac{14}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd} & =14\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft} \\\\ 4\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{yd} & =14\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $14\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $168\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{2}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$"] [17592186192566 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the quotient and remainder:\n\n- To find the quotient, we list the multiples of $8$ until we get to one that's larger than $62{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\times 8 & =8 \\\\ 2\\times 8 & =16 \\\\  & ⋮ \\\\ {\\color{red}{7}}\\times 8 & =56\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 8\\times 8 & =64\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, the quotient is ${\\color{red}{7}}$, and we can write the following:\n$62 \\div 8 = {\\color{red}{7}}\\,\\,\\textrm R\\,\\,\\bbox[3pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{blue}?}$\n- To find the remainder, we write the last equation as a multiplication plus the remainder:\n$62 = 8 \\times {\\color{red}{7}} \\,\\,+ \\,\\,\\bbox[3pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{blue}?}$\nThis equation simplifies as follows:\n$62 = 56 \\,\\,+ \\,\\,\\bbox[3pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{blue}?}$\n\nTherefore, the remainder must be ${\\color{blue}{6}}$. Finally, we have\n\n$$\n62 \\div 8 = {\\color{red}7} \\, \\textrm{R} \\, {\\color{blue}6}\n$$"] [17592186192567 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the quotient and remainder:\n\n- To find the quotient, we list the multiples of $6$ until we get to one that's larger than $51{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\times 6 & =6 \\\\ 2\\times 6 & =12 \\\\ 3\\times 6 & =18 \\\\ 4\\times 6 & =24 \\\\ 5\\times 6 & =30 \\\\ 6\\times 6 & =36 \\\\ 7\\times 6 & =42 \\\\ {\\color{red}{8}}\\times 6 & =48\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 9\\times 6 & =54\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, the quotient is ${\\color{red}{8}}$, and we can write the following:\n$51 \\div 6 = {\\color{red}{8}}\\,\\,\\textrm R\\,\\,\\bbox[3pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{blue}?}$\n- To find the remainder, we write the last equation as a multiplication plus the remainder:\n$51 = 6 \\times {\\color{red}{8}} \\,\\,+ \\,\\,\\bbox[3pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{blue}?}$\nThis equation simplifies as follows:\n$51 = 48 \\,\\,+ \\,\\,\\bbox[3pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{blue}?}$\n\nTherefore, the remainder must be ${\\color{blue}{3}}$. Finally, we have\n\n$$\n51 \\div 6 = {\\color{red}8} \\, \\textrm{R} \\, {\\color{blue}3}\n$$"] [17592186192604 :question/worked-solution "To construct a confidence interval, we start with a random variable that depends on the samples and has a known distribution under certain conditions.\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =\\frac{(\\overset{―}{X}-\\overset{―}{Y})-\\mu }{\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\overset{―}{X}-\\overset{―}{Y})-\\mu }{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\overset{―}{X}-\\overset{―}{Y})-\\mu }{({\\color{black}{\\frac{1}{3}}})},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n\nIt is known that if the samples are paired and the differences between each pair are normally distributed with a population mean $\\mu$ and known population variance, $Z$ has a standard normal distribution.\n\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n\nWe wish to find an interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/ce/ce18d66a53578e13b52bc7d6b6fd44ab6320ed98795be5621ae892727e7ef8eb.png)\n\nSince we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval must be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1 - 0.95}{2} = 0.025\n$$\n\nWe can find the endpoints of such interval using a table of values for the normal distribution.\n\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{aligned}-{z}_{0.025} & <Z<{z}_{0.025} \\\\ -{z}_{0.025} & <\\frac{(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-\\mu }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}<{z}_{0.025}\\end{aligned}$\n\nTo solve this inequality, we first multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nto get rid of the denominator:\n\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply all sides by $-1$:\n\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}$:\n\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite our inequality so that the smaller terms are on the left-hand side.\n\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we write our result more compactly.\n\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Consider the random variable\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =\\frac{(\\overset{―}{X}-\\overset{―}{Y})-\\mu }{\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\overset{―}{X}-\\overset{―}{Y})-\\mu }{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{27}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\overset{―}{X}-\\overset{―}{Y})-\\mu }{({\\color{black}{\\frac{1}{3}}})},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> where $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means of the given samples.\n> We have that\n> $Z \\sim {\\color{black}N(0,1)}$.\n> Using a table of values for the normal distribution, we require a value $c$ such that\n> $P(Z > c) = {\\color{black}0.025}$.\n> In other words,\n> $P(-c < Z < c) = 0.95$.\n> Let's call $c = z_{0.025}$.\n> Now, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the difference mean $\\mu$.\n> $\\begin{aligned}-{z}_{0.025} & <Z<{z}_{0.025} \\\\ -{z}_{0.025} & <\\frac{(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-\\mu }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}<{z}_{0.025}\\end{aligned}$\n> We solve the inequality as follows:\n> $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < (x^(―) - y^(―)) - μ < \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid > μ - (x^(―) - y^(―)) > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―) \\mid > μ > - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} + x^(―) - y^(―)\n$$\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x^(―) - y^(―) - \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3} \\mid < μ < x^(―) - y^(―) + \\frac{z_{0.025}}{3}\n$$\n> Finally, we can write our interval as follows:\n> $(x^(―) - y^(―)) ± z_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}$"] [17592186192621 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\lambda$ be the average number of customers visiting the restaurant per hour. Then, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{0}: λ &= 7 \\\\\n&= H_{1}: λ \\mid \\ne 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe critical region is the set of values of $X$ for which we reject the null hypothesis.\n\nThis is a two-sided test. So, to reject the null hypothesis, we need to observe an abnormally large or abnormally small value of $X$.\n\nTo reject the null hypothesis at a significance level of $5\\%$, we must observe a value $x$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq x) = 0.025\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq x) = 0.025\n$$\n\nunder the null hypothesis.\n\nUsing the table, we have the following probabilities:\n\n- For the left tail:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0) & =0.0009<0.025\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ P(X\\le 1) & =0.0073<0.025\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ P(X\\le 2) & =0.0296≮0.025\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the right tail:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 15) & =1-P(X\\le 14) \\\\  & =1-0.9943 \\\\  & =0.0057 \\\\  & <0.025\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ P(X\\ge 14) & =1-P(X\\le 13) \\\\  & =1-0.9872 \\\\  & =0.0128 \\\\  & <0.025\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ P(X\\ge 13) & =1-P(X\\le 12) \\\\  & =1-0.9730 \\\\  & =0.0270 \\\\  & ≮0.025\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the values of $X$ that would cause the null hypothesis to be rejected at a significance level of $5\\%$ are the values such that\n\n$$\nX \\leq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX \\geq 14\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\leq 1\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nX\\geq 14\n$$"]}
